# Master Textbook Structured Repository for all 38 Chapters
MASTER_TEXTBOOK_DATA = {('EM', 0): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Discrete Mathematics - Mathematical Logic & Set Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Analogy: The Wi-Fi Login & Father's Promise", 'text': "• Proposition: Like entering a Wi-Fi password—it is strictly Correct (True) or Incorrect (False). Questions or commands are not propositions.\n• Implication (P -> Q): Dad promises 'If you get AIR 1 (P), I will buy you a bike (Q)'. He only breaks his word if you get AIR 1 and he refuses to buy the bike (T -> F = False). If you don't get AIR 1, whatever he does never breaks the promise (Vacuous Truth)."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '1.1', 'title': 'Propositions & Basic Logic Connectives', 'content': "• <b>What is a Proposition?</b> A declarative sentence that is either strictly True (T) or strictly False (F), but never both.<br/>• <b>The 5 Fundamental Connectives:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>NOT (~P):</b> Reverses the truth value. If P is True, ~P is False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>AND (P ∧ Q):</b> True ONLY when BOTH P and Q are True (conjunction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>OR (P ∨ Q):</b> True if AT LEAST ONE of P or Q is True. False only when both are False (disjunction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Conditional / Implication (P → Q):</b> Reads 'If P, then Q'. False ONLY when P is True and Q is False.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Biconditional (P ↔ Q):</b> True when P and Q have identical truth values (both True or both False).<br/>• <b>Equivalence Identity:</b> (P → Q) is logically equivalent to (~P ∨ Q).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: P → Q is False ONLY when P = True and Q = False. In all other three cases (T→T, F→T, F→F), the implication is ALWAYS TRUE!'}, {'number': '1.2', 'title': 'Contrapositive, Converse, and Inverse', 'content': "• For any conditional statement <b>P → Q</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Contrapositive (~Q → ~P):</b> 'If NOT Q, then NOT P'.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Equivalence:</b> (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Always holds true in every possible case!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Converse (Q → P):</b> 'If Q, then P'. NOT equivalent to the original statement.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Inverse (~P → ~Q):</b> 'If NOT P, then NOT Q'. Equivalent to the Converse, NOT the original.<br/>• <b>GATE Shortcut:</b> When proving an implication theorem in GATE, proving its contrapositive is often 10x easier!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A statement and its Contrapositive always share the exact same truth table: (P → Q) ≡ (~Q → ~P). Converse and Inverse are equal to each other, but NOT to the original statement.'}, {'number': '1.3', 'title': 'Quantifiers: For All (∀) vs There Exists (∃)', 'content': "• <b>Universal Quantifier (∀x):</b> 'For all x'. True only if the statement holds for every single element in the universe.<br/>• <b>Existential Quantifier (∃x):</b> 'There exists x'. True if at least one matching element exists in the universe.<br/>• <b>Negation Rules (De Morgan for Quantifiers):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ~[ ∀x P(x) ] ≡ ∃x [ ~P(x) ] (Negating 'All birds fly' gives 'There exists at least one bird that does NOT fly').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ~[ ∃x P(x) ] ≡ ∀x [ ~P(x) ] (Negating 'Someone got 100' gives 'Everyone scored not 100').<br/>• <b>Order of Quantifiers Matters:</b> ∀x ∃y P(x, y) is NOT the same as ∃y ∀x P(x, y)!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Negating 'All X are Y' is 'There is at least one X that is NOT Y'. It is NEVER 'No X are Y'!"}, {'number': '1.4', 'title': 'Counting Relations on a Set of Size n', 'content': '• Let set A contain <b>n</b> elements. The Cartesian product A × A contains <b>n²</b> ordered pairs.<br/>• <b>Formulas to memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Total possible relations = <b>2^(n²)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Total Reflexive relations = <b>2^(n² - n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Symmetric relations = <b>2^(n(n+1)/2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Total Reflexive and Symmetric relations = <b>2^(n(n-1)/2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. Total Equivalence relations = Counted by <b>Bell Numbers</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;B(1) = 1, B(2) = 2, B(3) = 5, B(4) = 15, B(5) = 52.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For n = 4 elements, total equivalence relations = Bell Number B(4) = 15. Memorize: B(1)=1, B(2)=2, B(3)=5, B(4)=15, B(5)=52.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Counting Symmetric Relations for n = 4', 'problem': 'Given set S = {a, b, c, d}. How many symmetric relations can be defined on set S?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify number of elements n = 4.', 'Step 2: Formula for total symmetric relations is 2^(n(n+1)/2).', 'Step 3: Exponent = (4 × (4 + 1)) / 2 = (4 × 5) / 2 = 20 / 2 = 10.', 'Step 4: Compute 2^10 = 1024.'], 'answer': 'Total Symmetric Relations = 1024.'}], 'exam_traps': ['A relation can be BOTH symmetric and anti-symmetric at the same time (e.g. Identity relation on any set)!', 'Empty relation on an empty set is reflexive, but empty relation on a non-empty set is NOT reflexive.', 'Vacuous truth trap: The empty relation is vacuously symmetric and transitive!']}, ('EM', 1): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Combinatorics, Recurrence Relations & Graph Theory', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Socks in the Dark & Network Handshakes', 'text': '• Pigeonhole Principle: If a drawer has black and blue socks, pulling out 3 socks guarantees at least 1 matching pair because 3 items go into 2 categories (color buckets).\n• Handshaking Theorem: At any party, when two people shake hands, 1 handshake adds 2 to the sum of handshakes. That is why the sum of degrees in any graph is always an even number!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '2.1', 'title': 'Pigeonhole Principle (PHP) & Permutations', 'content': '• <b>Basic PHP:</b> If <b>k + 1</b> or more objects are placed into <b>k</b> boxes, then at least one box must contain 2 or more objects.<br/>• <b>Generalized PHP:</b> If <b>N</b> objects are placed into <b>k</b> boxes, then at least one box contains at least <b>⌈N / k⌉</b> objects.<br/>• <b>Permutations vs Combinations:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Permutation (Order matters): P(n, r) = n! / (n - r)!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Combination (Order does NOT matter): C(n, r) = n! / [ r! (n - r)! ]<br/>• <b>Circular Permutations:</b> Arranging n distinct objects around a circular table = <b>(n - 1)!</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To guarantee at least m items in the same box out of k boxes, you need at least k(m - 1) + 1 items in total!'}, {'number': '2.2', 'title': 'Solving Linear Recurrence Relations', 'content': '• <b>Homogeneous Recurrence:</b> a_n = c1·a_{n-1} + c2·a_{n-2}.<br/>• <b>Step-by-step Solution:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Form characteristic equation: r² - c1·r - c2 = 0.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 1 (Distinct Real Roots r1 ≠ r2):</b> General solution is a_n = A·(r1)^n + B·(r2)^n.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 2 (Repeated Roots r1 = r2 = r):</b> General solution is a_n = (A + B·n)·r^n.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Substitute base cases (a0, a1) to solve for constants A and B.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If characteristic root r has multiplicity m, the solution term is (A0 + A1·n + ... + A_{m-1}·n^{m-1})·r^n.'}, {'number': '2.3', 'title': 'Graph Theory Fundamentals & Handshaking Theorem', 'content': '• <b>Handshaking Lemma:</b> In any undirected graph: <b>∑ deg(v) = 2|E|</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum of all vertex degrees is always EVEN.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Corollary:</b> In every graph, the number of vertices with ODD degree is ALWAYS EVEN!<br/>• <b>Complete Graph (K_n):</b> Every pair of vertices has an edge. Total edges = <b>n(n - 1) / 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Bipartite Graph (K_{m, n}):</b> Vertices split into two independent sets. Max edges = <b>m × n</b>.<br/>• <b>Cycle Graph (C_n):</b> Regular graph of degree 2 with n vertices and n edges.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: A graph is bipartite if and only if it contains NO odd-length cycles! If a graph has a triangle (C_3) or pentagon (C_5), it CANNOT be bipartite.'}, {'number': '2.4', 'title': 'Planar Graphs & Spanning Trees', 'content': "• <b>Euler's Planar Formula:</b> For any connected planar graph with V vertices, E edges, and R regions:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>V - E + R = 2</b>.<br/>• <b>Planarity Edge Bounds:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For any connected planar graph with V ≥ 3: <b>E ≤ 3V - 6</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If triangle-free (no cycle of length 3): <b>E ≤ 2V - 4</b>.<br/>• <b>Kuratowski's Theorem:</b> A graph is planar if and only if it does not contain a subgraph homeomorphic to K_5 or K_{3,3}.<br/>• <b>Trees:</b> A connected acyclic graph on n vertices has exactly <b>n - 1 edges</b>. Cayley's formula: Total labeled trees on n vertices = <b>n^(n - 2)</b>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: K_5 (complete graph on 5 vertices) and K_{3,3} (complete bipartite utility graph) are the two minimal non-planar graphs! E ≤ 3V - 6 is a necessary condition for planarity, not sufficient.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Planar Graph Regions Calculation', 'problem': 'A connected planar graph has 10 vertices and each vertex has degree 3. How many faces (regions) does this graph divide the plane into?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Use Handshaking Theorem to find E: ∑ deg(v) = 2E.', 'Step 2: Here 10 vertices each have degree 3. Total degree sum = 10 × 3 = 30.', 'Step 3: 2E = 30 ⇒ E = 15 edges.', "Step 4: Apply Euler's Planar Formula: V - E + R = 2.", 'Step 5: Substitute V = 10, E = 15: 10 - 15 + R = 2 ⇒ -5 + R = 2 ⇒ R = 7.'], 'answer': 'Total Regions (Faces) R = 7.'}], 'exam_traps': ["Euler's formula V - E + R = 2 requires the graph to be CONNECTED. If disconnected with k components: V - E + R = 1 + k.", 'Bounded regions vs unbounded regions: R includes the 1 outer infinite region! Bounded faces = R - 1.']}, ('EM', 2): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Linear Algebra - Matrices, Systems of Equations & Eigenvalues', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: 3D Graphics Rotation & Bridge Resonance', 'text': '• Matrices: In video games, moving and rotating a 3D camera is simply multiplying 3D coordinate vectors by 4x4 transformation matrices.\n• Eigenvalues: The natural vibration frequencies of a bridge or building. When earthquake vibrations match an eigenvalue, catastrophic resonance occurs!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '3.1', 'title': 'Matrix Determinants, Rank & Inverses', 'content': '• <b>Rank of a Matrix ρ(A):</b> Maximum number of linearly independent rows (or columns).<br/>• <b>Key Rank Properties:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For an m × n matrix: <b>ρ(A) ≤ min(m, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ρ(A × B) ≤ min(ρ(A), ρ(B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- ρ(A + B) ≤ ρ(A) + ρ(B).<br/>• <b>Inverse Condition:</b> A square matrix A is invertible (non-singular) if and only if <b>det(A) ≠ 0</b> (full rank).<br/>• <b>Determinant Properties:</b> det(A × B) = det(A) · det(B); det(k · A_{n×n}) = k^n · det(A); det(A^T) = det(A).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For an n × n matrix, scaling all elements by constant k multiplies determinant by k^n: det(k·A) = k^n · det(A)!'}, {'number': '3.2', 'title': 'System of Linear Equations (AX = B) Consistency', 'content': '• Consider system <b>AX = B</b> with n variables. Let [A | B] be the augmented matrix.<br/>• <b>Step-by-Step Consistency Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Inconsistent (No Solution):</b> If <b>ρ(A) ≠ ρ(A | B)</b> (i.e. ρ(A) < ρ(A | B)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Consistent (Unique Solution):</b> If <b>ρ(A) = ρ(A | B) = n</b> (full rank equal to number of unknowns).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Consistent (Infinitely Many Solutions):</b> If <b>ρ(A) = ρ(A | B) = r < n</b>. Number of free variables = <b>n - r</b>.<br/>• <b>Homogeneous System (AX = 0):</b> Always consistent (trivial solution X = 0). Has non-trivial solutions if and only if <b>det(A) = 0</b> (ρ(A) < n).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Homogeneous system AX = 0 has non-zero (non-trivial) solutions IF AND ONLY IF det(A) = 0 (rows are linearly dependent).'}, {'number': '3.3', 'title': 'Eigenvalues & Eigenvectors (The 5 Golden Properties)', 'content': '• Characteristic equation: <b>det(A - λI) = 0</b>.<br/>• <b>The 5 Golden Exam Properties:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sum of Eigenvalues:</b> Equal to the <b>Trace of A</b> (sum of main diagonal elements): ∑ λ_i = Tr(A).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Product of Eigenvalues:</b> Equal to the <b>Determinant of A</b>: ∏ λ_i = det(A).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Triangular Matrices:</b> For upper triangular, lower triangular, or diagonal matrices, eigenvalues are SIMPLY the main diagonal elements!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. If λ is an eigenvalue of A, then:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^k has eigenvalue <b>λ^k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- A^(-1) has eigenvalue <b>1 / λ</b> (if λ ≠ 0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- (A + cI) has eigenvalue <b>λ + c</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Symmetric Matrices:</b> Real symmetric matrices always have strictly REAL eigenvalues.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 90% of GATE eigenvalue questions, you DO NOT need to solve the cubic equation! Just check Trace = Sum(λ) and Det = Product(λ) against the 4 options!'}, {'number': '3.4', 'title': 'Cayley-Hamilton Theorem & Matrix Powers', 'content': '• <b>Theorem Statement:</b> Every square matrix satisfies its own characteristic equation.<br/>• If characteristic equation is <b>λ² - 5λ + 6 = 0</b>, then matrix satisfies: <b>A² - 5A + 6I = 0</b>.<br/>• <b>Applications in GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Finding A^(-1):</b> Multiply equation by A^(-1): A - 5I + 6A^(-1) = 0 ⇒ <b>A^(-1) = (5I - A) / 6</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Computing High Powers (A^8, A^100):</b> Reduce high polynomial powers modulo the characteristic polynomial!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Multiply the characteristic equation by A^(-1) to obtain the inverse without ever computing cofactors or adjoints!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Eigenvalues using Trace and Determinant', 'problem': 'Find the eigenvalues of matrix A = [[2, 1], [1, 2]].', 'steps': ['Step 1: Calculate Trace(A) = sum of main diagonal = 2 + 2 = 4.', 'Step 2: Calculate Det(A) = (2 × 2) - (1 × 1) = 4 - 1 = 3.', 'Step 3: Two eigenvalues λ1, λ2 satisfy: λ1 + λ2 = 4 and λ1 · λ2 = 3.', 'Step 4: Two numbers that add to 4 and multiply to 3 are 1 and 3.'], 'answer': 'Eigenvalues are λ1 = 3, λ2 = 1.'}], 'exam_traps': ['det(A + B) is NOT equal to det(A) + det(B)!', 'Eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly independent, but eigenvectors for repeated eigenvalues may or may not be independent.']}, ('EM', 3): {'sub_id': 'EM', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': "Calculus, Probability Distributions & Bayes' Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Medical Test Accuracy & Coin Flips', 'text': "• Bayes' Theorem: A medical test is 99% accurate, but a disease is rare (1 in 10,000). If you test positive, the chance you actually have the disease is only ~1%! Because false positives from the healthy 9,999 vastly outnumber true positives.\n• Poisson Distribution: Modeling rare events over time, such as server web requests per minute or customer arrivals at an ATM."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '4.1', 'title': "Limits & L'Hôpital's Rule", 'content': "• <b>L'Hôpital's Rule:</b> Applicable ONLY for indeterminate forms <b>0 / 0</b> or <b>±∞ / ±∞</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;lim_{x→a} [ f(x) / g(x) ] = lim_{x→a} [ f'(x) / g'(x) ].<br/>• <b>Key Standard Limits for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{x→0} [ sin(x) / x ] = 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{x→0} [ (e^x - 1) / x ] = 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{x→0} [ ln(1 + x) / x ] = 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- lim_{n→∞} [ 1 + (a / n) ]^n = <b>e^a</b>.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Always verify that the expression actually evaluates to 0/0 or ∞/∞ before taking derivatives! Applying L'Hôpital to a determinate form (like 0/1) gives completely wrong answers."}, {'number': '4.2', 'title': 'Maxima, Minima & Mean Value Theorems', 'content': "• <b>Finding Critical Points:</b> Set first derivative f'(x) = 0.<br/>• <b>Second Derivative Test:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If f''(x) < 0: Local <b>Maximum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If f''(x) > 0: Local <b>Minimum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If f''(x) = 0: Test inconclusive (inflection point candidate).<br/>• <b>Rolle's Theorem:</b> If f(x) is continuous on [a, b] and differentiable on (a, b), with f(a) = f(b), then there exists at least one c ∈ (a, b) where <b>f'(c) = 0</b>.<br/>• <b>Lagrange Mean Value Theorem (LMVT):</b> f'(c) = [ f(b) - f(a) ] / (b - a).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a closed interval [a, b], global absolute extrema can occur at critical points inside OR at the interval endpoints x = a and x = b. Always check endpoints!'}, {'number': '4.3', 'title': "Conditional Probability & Bayes' Theorem", 'content': "• <b>Conditional Probability:</b> P(A | B) = P(A ∩ B) / P(B).<br/>• <b>Independent Events:</b> Events A and B are independent if and only if <b>P(A ∩ B) = P(A) · P(B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Bayes' Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>P(A_i | B) = [ P(B | A_i) · P(A_i) ] / ∑ [ P(B | A_j) · P(A_j) ]</b>.<br/>• <b>Mutually Exclusive vs Independent:</b> If two non-zero events are mutually exclusive (P(A ∩ B) = 0), they CANNOT be independent!", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Mutually exclusive means they cannot happen together (disjoint). Independent means occurrence of one does not affect the probability of the other. They are completely different concepts!'}, {'number': '4.4', 'title': 'Probability Distributions (Binomial, Poisson, Normal)', 'content': '• <b>Binomial Distribution:</b> P(X = k) = C(n, k) · p^k · (1 - p)^{n - k}.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Mean = <b>n · p</b>; Variance = <b>n · p · (1 - p)</b>.<br/>• <b>Poisson Distribution:</b> Rare events with rate λ: P(X = k) = (e^(-λ) · λ^k) / k!.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Unique Property:</b> Mean = <b>λ</b> and Variance = <b>λ</b> (Mean equals Variance!).<br/>• <b>Exponential Distribution:</b> Continuous waiting time: f(x) = λ·e^(-λx).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Memoryless property: P(X > s + t | X > s) = P(X > t).<br/>• <b>Uniform Distribution on [a, b]:</b> Mean = (a + b) / 2; Variance = <b>(b - a)² / 12</b>.', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Poisson distribution, Mean = Variance = λ. If an exam question says 'the variance of a Poisson variable is 4', the mean is immediately 4 and λ = 4!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Bayes' Theorem in Defective Item Screening", 'problem': 'Machine A produces 60% of items with 2% defect rate. Machine B produces 40% of items with 4% defect rate. An item is selected at random and found defective. What is the probability it was produced by Machine B?', 'steps': ['Step 1: P(A) = 0.60, P(B) = 0.40.', 'Step 2: P(D | A) = 0.02, P(D | B) = 0.04.', 'Step 3: Total Probability of defective item P(D) = P(A)·P(D|A) + P(B)·P(D|B) = (0.60 × 0.02) + (0.40 × 0.04) = 0.012 + 0.016 = 0.028.', "Step 4: By Bayes' Theorem: P(B | D) = [ P(B) · P(D | B) ] / P(D) = 0.016 / 0.028 = 16 / 28 = 4 / 7 ≈ 0.5714."], 'answer': 'P(Machine B | Defective) = 4/7 (approx 57.14%).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Variance can NEVER be negative! Var(X) = E[X²] - (E[X])² ≥ 0.', 'Scaling property: Var(aX + b) = a² · Var(X). The constant b adds zero to variance!']}, ('DL', 0): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Number Systems, Signed Numbers & IEEE-754 Floating Point', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Odometer Wrap-Around & Game Integer Overflow', 'text': "• 2's Complement: Like a car odometer running backwards from 00000 to 99999. In an 8-bit computer, counting backwards from 00000000 gives 11111111, which represents -1.\n• Overflow: In early arcade games, when a score reached 255 (max for 8 bits) and added 1, it rolled over to 0! That is integer overflow."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '5.1', 'title': 'Signed Number Ranges for n bits', 'content': "• For an <b>n-bit</b> signed integer representation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Unsigned:</b> Range is <b>0 to 2^n - 1</b> (all bits used for magnitude).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Sign-Magnitude:</b> Range is <b>-(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Has two representations of zero: +0 and -0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>1's Complement:</b> Range is <b>-(2^(n-1) - 1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Also has two zeros: +0 and -0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>2's Complement:</b> Range is <b>-2^(n-1) to +(2^(n-1) - 1)</b>. (Has only ONE unique zero!).<br/>• <b>Asymmetry in 2's Complement:</b> For n = 8 bits, range is <b>-128 to +127</b>. Notice -128 has no positive counterpart in 8 bits!", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 2's complement with n bits, the minimum negative number is -2^(n-1). It has NO positive counterpart (+2^(n-1) causes overflow). To find 2's complement fast: invert all bits and add 1, OR copy bits from right to left up to the first '1', then flip all remaining bits!"}, {'number': '5.2', 'title': "2's Complement Overflow Detection", 'content': '• <b>When does Overflow occur?</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding two POSITIVE numbers gives a NEGATIVE result.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding two NEGATIVE numbers gives a POSITIVE result.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Adding a positive and a negative number NEVER causes overflow!<br/>• <b>Hardware Detection Rule:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Overflow = C_in ⊕ C_out</b> (XOR of carry-in and carry-out of the most significant bit MSB).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If C_in ≠ C_out, overflow has definitely occurred!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In signed arithmetic, carry-out from MSB does NOT mean overflow! Overflow occurs if and only if C_{in_MSB} ⊕ C_{out_MSB} = 1.'}, {'number': '5.3', 'title': 'IEEE-754 Single Precision (32-bit) Floating Point', 'content': '• The 32 bits are divided into 3 distinct fields:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Sign bit (S):</b> 1 bit (0 for positive, 1 for negative).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Exponent (E):</b> 8 bits with <b>Bias = 127</b>. Stored E = actual exponent e + 127.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Mantissa / Fraction (M):</b> 23 bits representing fractional part with implicit leading 1: <b>1.M</b>.<br/>• <b>Value Formula:</b> (-1)^S × 1.M × 2^(E - 127).<br/>• <b>Special Bit Patterns:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 255 and M = 0: Represents <b>±Infinity</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 255 and M ≠ 0: Represents <b>NaN (Not a Number)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 0 and M = 0: Represents <b>±0</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If E = 0 and M ≠ 0: <b>Denormalized number</b> with value (-1)^S × 0.M × 2^(-126).', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: For normalized IEEE-754 numbers, the leading '1' before the decimal point is implicit (not stored in memory). The smallest normalized positive number is 1.0 × 2^(-126) (E = 1)."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: IEEE-754 Representation of -13.625', 'problem': 'Convert the real decimal number -13.625 into IEEE-754 single precision 32-bit binary representation.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Sign is negative ⇒ S = 1.', 'Step 2: Convert integer part: 13 = 1101 in binary.', 'Step 3: Convert fractional part: 0.625 = 0.5 + 0.125 = 0.101 in binary.', 'Step 4: Combine: 13.625 = 1101.101 in binary.', 'Step 5: Normalize to 1.M form: 1101.101 = 1.101101 × 2^3.', 'Step 6: Actual exponent e = 3. Stored exponent E = 3 + 127 = 130 = 10000010 in binary.', 'Step 7: Mantissa M = 101101 padded with zeros to 23 bits.', 'Step 8: Combine (S | E | M): 1 | 10000010 | 10110100000000000000000. In Hex: C15A0000.'], 'answer': 'Hex representation: 0xC15A0000.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Forgetting to add bias 127 to the exponent!', 'Double precision IEEE-754 uses 64 bits: 1 sign bit, 11 exponent bits (bias = 1023), and 52 mantissa bits.']}, ('DL', 1): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Boolean Algebra, Logic Gates & K-Map Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Universal Factory Tool & Logic Simplification', 'text': '• Universal Gates: Just as LEGO bricks can build any toy castle or spaceship, NAND and NOR gates alone can build any computer chip in existence!\n• K-Map: A visual puzzle where adjacent boxes differ by only 1 bit. Grouping neighboring 1s cancels out changing variables, shrinking circuit size.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '6.1', 'title': 'Boolean Algebra Laws & Consensus Theorem', 'content': "• <b>De Morgan's Laws:</b> (A + B)' = A' · B' &nbsp;and&nbsp; (A · B)' = A' + B'.<br/>• <b>Distributive Law:</b> A + BC = (A + B)(A + C). (Crucial for factoring expressions!).<br/>• <b>Consensus Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Variable A appears in true form and complemented form (A, A').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- The term <b>BC</b> is redundant (consensus of AB and A'C) and can be completely eliminated!<br/>• <b>Dual Consensus Form:</b> (A + B)(A' + C)(B + C) = (A + B)(A' + C).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In consensus theorem AB + A'C + BC = AB + A'C, look for the variable that appears complemented (A and A'). The term formed by the remaining two variables (BC) is ALWAYS REDUNDANT!"}, {'number': '6.2', 'title': 'Universal Gates: Minimum NAND/NOR Implementation Counts', 'content': '• NAND and NOR are universal gates because any boolean function can be realized using only NAND or only NOR.<br/>• <b>Minimum Gate Counts to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>NOT:</b> 1 NAND | 1 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>AND:</b> 2 NAND | 3 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>OR:</b> 3 NAND | 2 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>XOR:</b> 4 NAND | 5 NOR<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>XNOR:</b> 5 NAND | 4 NOR', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: XOR requires 4 NAND gates (or 5 NOR gates). XNOR requires 5 NAND gates (or 4 NOR gates). Notice the perfect symmetry: 4 vs 5!'}, {'number': '6.3', 'title': 'K-Map Minimization, Prime & Essential Prime Implicants', 'content': "• <b>Gray Code Ordering:</b> Cells in K-map are ordered 00, 01, 11, 10 so adjacent cells differ by exactly 1 bit.<br/>• <b>Implicant:</b> Any product term that covers one or more 1s of the function.<br/>• <b>Prime Implicant (PI):</b> A rectangle of size 2^k (1, 2, 4, 8, 16) that CANNOT be merged into a larger group.<br/>• <b>Essential Prime Implicant (EPI):</b> A Prime Implicant that covers at least one '1' that is NOT covered by any other prime implicant.<br/>• <b>Minimal SOP expression MUST contain ALL Essential Prime Implicants!</b>", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every EPI is a PI, but not every PI is an EPI. To find EPIs fast: find a '1' that belongs to only ONE valid largest rectangle. That rectangle is an Essential Prime Implicant!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Boolean Function Minimization using Consensus', 'problem': "Simplify the Boolean function F = X'Y + XZ + YZ.", 'steps': ["Step 1: Check the structure of terms: X'Y, XZ, YZ.", "Step 2: Variable X appears complemented as X and X'.", 'Step 3: The other two variables are Y and Z.', "Step 4: The third term YZ is the consensus of X'Y and XZ.", "Step 5: By Consensus Theorem, YZ is redundant and eliminated: F = X'Y + XZ."], 'answer': "Simplified function: F = X'Y + XZ."}], 'exam_traps': ["In K-maps, grouping 0s gives the minimized POS expression (F'), not SOP!", "Don't-care conditions (X) can be included in groups to make groups larger, but you NEVER need to cover all don't-cares."]}, ('DL', 2): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Combinational Circuits - Multiplexers, Decoders & Adders', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Train Track Switch & Address Decoder', 'text': '• Multiplexer (MUX): Like a railway switch operator who chooses which incoming track (data line) connects to the single outgoing main line, controlled by switch levers (select lines).\n• Decoder: Like a postal delivery worker who reads a numeric zip code and delivers the package to exactly one specific house door.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '7.1', 'title': 'Multiplexer (MUX) as a Universal Circuit', 'content': "• A <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b> has 2^n data inputs, n select lines, and 1 output.<br/>• <b>Implementing any n-variable function:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Using a <b>2^n : 1 MUX</b>: Connect all n variables directly to select lines. Connect truth table outputs (0 or 1) to data inputs. No extra gates needed!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Using a <b>2^(n-1) : 1 MUX</b>: Connect (n - 1) variables to select lines. The last variable X can provide data inputs as <b>0, 1, X, or X'</b>. May need an inverter for X'.<br/>• <b>Shannon's Expansion Theorem:</b> F(A, B, C) = A' · F(0, B, C) + A · F(1, B, C).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Any Boolean function of n variables can be implemented using a single 2^(n-1) : 1 MUX with at most one external inverter!'}, {'number': '7.2', 'title': 'Half Adder vs Full Adder Architecture', 'content': '• <b>Half Adder (HA):</b> Adds 2 single-bit numbers A and B.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <b>A ⊕ B</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry = <b>A · B</b>.<br/>• <b>Full Adder (FA):</b> Adds 3 bits: A, B, and Carry-in C_in.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Sum = <b>A ⊕ B ⊕ C_in</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Carry-out = <b>AB + BC_in + AC_in = AB + C_in(A ⊕ B)</b>.<br/>• <b>Construction:</b> 1 Full Adder can be constructed using exactly <b>2 Half Adders and 1 OR gate</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To build an n-bit Ripple Carry Adder, you need 1 Half Adder and (n - 1) Full Adders (or n Full Adders if initial carry is needed). Total delay = n × (carry propagation delay).'}, {'number': '7.3', 'title': 'Decoders & Priority Encoders', 'content': '• <b>n-to-2^n Decoder:</b> Converts n binary inputs into 2^n active minterm outputs.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any Boolean function can be implemented using a Decoder and an external OR gate (sum of minterms).<br/>• <b>Priority Encoder:</b> If multiple inputs are active simultaneously, the output represents the highest priority active input.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Includes a <b>Valid bit (V)</b>: V = 1 if at least one input is active; V = 0 if all inputs are inactive.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To construct an m-to-2^m decoder using smaller k-to-2^k decoders: Number of smaller decoders required = ⌈2^m / 2^k⌉ + tree levels.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Implementing Logic with 4:1 MUX', 'problem': 'Implement F(A, B, C) = ∑m(1, 2, 4, 7) using a 4:1 MUX where select lines S1 = A and S0 = B.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Write truth table for (A, B, C) and output F.', 'Step 2: Group by (A, B) pairs: (0,0), (0,1), (1,0), (1,1).', 'Step 3: For AB = 00: m0(C=0)->F=0, m1(C=1)->F=1 ⇒ I0 = C.', "Step 4: For AB = 01: m2(C=0)->F=1, m3(C=1)->F=0 ⇒ I1 = C'.", "Step 5: For AB = 10: m4(C=0)->F=1, m5(C=1)->F=0 ⇒ I2 = C'.", 'Step 6: For AB = 11: m6(C=0)->F=0, m7(C=1)->F=1 ⇒ I3 = C.'], 'answer': "Inputs to 4:1 MUX are I0 = C, I1 = C', I2 = C', I3 = C."}], 'exam_traps': ['Pay strict attention to which variable is MSB and LSB on the select lines (S1 = A, S0 = B vs S1 = B, S0 = A)!', 'Active-low enables in decoders require invert logic before cascading.']}, ('DL', 3): {'sub_id': 'DL', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Sequential Circuits, Flip-Flops & Counters', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Stop-Motion Camera & Turnstile Counter', 'text': "• Flip-Flop: Like a high-speed camera taking a photo strictly on the rising edge of the photographer's click. Inputs must stay stable before (setup time) and after (hold time) the snapshot.\n• Counter: Like an amusement park turnstile clicker that increments each time a visitor passes through, cycling back to zero when max capacity is reached."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '8.1', 'title': 'Flip-Flop Characteristic Equations & Truth Tables', 'content': "• <b>SR Flip-Flop:</b> Q_{next} = S + R'Q (Invalid state when S = 1, R = 1).<br/>• <b>JK Flip-Flop:</b> Q_{next} = J·Q' + K'·Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=0: Hold (Q).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=0, K=1: Reset (0).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=0: Set (1).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- J=1, K=1: Toggle (Q') - solves SR race condition!<br/>• <b>D Flip-Flop (Delay):</b> Q_{next} = <b>D</b> (data storage).<br/>• <b>T Flip-Flop (Toggle):</b> Q_{next} = <b>T ⊕ Q</b> (T=0: Hold, T=1: Toggle).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: The characteristic equations must be on your fingertips: JK: Q_{next} = J·Q' + K'·Q | D: Q_{next} = D | T: Q_{next} = T ⊕ Q."}, {'number': '8.2', 'title': 'Timing Constraints: Setup Time, Hold Time & Max Clock Frequency', 'content': '• <b>Setup Time (t_setup):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable BEFORE the active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Hold Time (t_hold):</b> Minimum time data input must remain stable AFTER the active clock edge.<br/>• <b>Minimum Clock Period (T_clk):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_clk ≥ t_{cq} + t_{comb} + t_{setup}</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- t_{cq} = Clock-to-Q delay of the launching flip-flop.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- t_{comb} = Maximum propagation delay of combinational logic between flip-flops.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- t_{setup} = Setup time of the receiving flip-flop.<br/>• <b>Maximum Clock Frequency:</b> f_{max} = 1 / T_{clk_min}.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To avoid Hold time violations: t_{cq_min} + t_{comb_min} ≥ t_{hold}. Notice that hold time is independent of clock frequency!'}, {'number': '8.3', 'title': 'Counters: Asynchronous vs Synchronous, Ring & Johnson', 'content': '• <b>Asynchronous (Ripple) Counter:</b> Output of one flip-flop acts as clock for the next.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Total propagation delay = <b>n × t_{pd}</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Max clock frequency decreases rapidly as bit-width n increases.<br/>• <b>Synchronous Counter:</b> All flip-flops share the same master clock. Much faster! Delay = <b>t_{pd} + t_{gate}</b>.<br/>• <b>Shift Register Counters with n Flip-Flops:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Standard Binary Counter:</b> Has <b>2^n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Ring Counter:</b> Output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>n</b> unique states.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Johnson (Twisted Ring) Counter:</b> Inverted output of last FF fed back to first. Has <b>2n</b> unique states!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An n-flip-flop Johnson counter has 2n states, while an n-flip-flop Ring counter has only n states! Unused states: Ring has (2^n - n) unused states; Johnson has (2^n - 2n) unused states.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Maximum Clock Frequency', 'problem': 'A sequential circuit has flip-flop t_cq = 2 ns, t_setup = 1.5 ns, and combinational logic delay = 4.5 ns. What is the maximum clock frequency f_max?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula for minimum clock period: T_clk ≥ t_cq + t_comb + t_setup.', 'Step 2: Substitute values: T_clk_min = 2 ns + 4.5 ns + 1.5 ns = 8 ns.', 'Step 3: Maximum frequency f_max = 1 / T_clk_min = 1 / (8 × 10^(-9) s) = 125 MHz.'], 'answer': 'Maximum clock frequency = 125 MHz.'}], 'exam_traps': ["In negative edge-triggered ripple counter: connecting Q to clock gives an UP counter; connecting Q' gives a DOWN counter!", 'Race-around condition in JK flip-flop occurs when J = 1, K = 1 and clock pulse width t_p > propagation delay t_pd in level-triggered latches. Solved by edge-triggering or master-slave configuration.']}, ('COA', 0): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Machine Instructions & Addressing Modes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Treasure Map Coordinates', 'text': '• Immediate Addressing: Cash in your pocket ($20 bill right now).\n• Direct Addressing: Mailing a letter to a specific house address (e.g. 104 Main St).\n• Indirect Addressing: A bank locker key inside a drawer. You open the drawer (first address) to find the key that opens the real locker (second address)!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '9.1', 'title': 'Effective Address (EA) in Common Addressing Modes', 'content': '• <b>What is Effective Address (EA)?</b> The actual physical address in main memory where the operand resides.<br/>• <b>Standard Addressing Modes Summary:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Immediate Mode:</b> Operand is part of the instruction itself. (Zero memory references!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Direct (Absolute) Mode:</b> Address field contains EA: <b>EA = Address</b>. (1 memory reference).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Indirect Mode:</b> Address field contains pointer to EA: <b>EA = M[Address]</b>. (2 memory references).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Register Mode:</b> Operand resides in a CPU register R: <b>EA = R</b>. (Zero memory references, very fast).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;5. <b>Register Indirect Mode:</b> Register R contains memory address: <b>EA = [R]</b>. (1 memory reference).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;6. <b>PC-Relative Mode:</b> Used in branch jumps: <b>EA = PC + Offset</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;7. <b>Indexed Mode:</b> Used in array traversal: <b>EA = Base Address + [Index Register]</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In PC-Relative mode, PC is already incremented to point to the NEXT instruction before computing EA = PC + Offset!'}, {'number': '9.2', 'title': 'Instruction Formats & Opcode Expansion', 'content': '• <b>Instruction Word Fields:</b> [ Opcode | Mode | Register Address | Memory Address ].<br/>• <b>Number of Addresses:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 3-Address: ADD R1, R2, R3 (R1 = R2 + R3).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 2-Address: ADD R1, R2 (R1 = R1 + R2).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 1-Address: ADD X (Accumulator AC = AC + M[X]).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 0-Address: ADD (Stack machine: pops top two operands, adds, pushes result).<br/>• <b>Expanding Opcode Technique:</b> Allows variable length opcodes to support many instructions with different address counts in a fixed word size.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In an expanding opcode question, unused bit patterns from k-bit opcodes are multiplied by 2^(extra bits) to calculate how many shorter-address instructions can be created.'}, {'number': '9.3', 'title': 'RISC vs CISC Architecture Differences', 'content': '• <b>RISC (Reduced Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Simple, single-cycle instructions with fixed instruction length.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Load/Store Architecture:</b> Arithmetic operations only occur between registers.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Hardwired control unit, heavily pipelined, large register file.<br/>• <b>CISC (Complex Instruction Set Computer):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Complex, multi-cycle instructions with variable instruction length.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Memory-to-memory operations allowed (e.g. ADD directly to memory).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Microprogrammed control unit with fewer general registers.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In RISC processors, ALU instructions CANNOT access memory directly! Data must be explicitly loaded into registers using LOAD/STORE instructions.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Memory References for Different Modes', 'problem': 'An instruction is stored at memory location 1000. It has opcode, mode, and address field 500. Memory at 500 contains 800, and memory at 800 contains 25. How many memory references are required to fetch and execute the instruction in Indirect Addressing Mode?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Instruction fetch requires 1 memory reference (read address 1000).', 'Step 2: Read address field 500 from memory to find effective address pointer: reads 800 (1 memory reference).', 'Step 3: Read effective address 800 from memory to fetch operand 25 (1 memory reference).', 'Step 4: Total memory references = 1 (fetch) + 2 (operand fetch) = 3 references.'], 'answer': 'Total memory references = 3 (1 for instruction fetch + 2 for operand fetch).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not forget the 1 memory reference needed to FETCH the instruction itself!', 'Immediate addressing requires 1 memory reference for instruction fetch, but 0 references for operand fetch.']}, ('COA', 1): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'ALU, Control Unit & Memory Interfacing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Speed Multiplication Trick & Direct Flight DMA', 'text': "• Booth's Algorithm: Like converting 99 × 7 into (100 - 1) × 7 = 700 - 7 = 693. Instead of adding 99 times, you subtract once and add once!\n• DMA (Direct Memory Access): Like a freight train delivering cargo straight to the warehouse (RAM) without asking the airport CEO (CPU) to inspect each individual parcel."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '10.1', 'title': "Booth's Multiplication Rules (Q_i, Q_{i-1})", 'content': "• Booth's Algorithm multiplies signed 2's complement numbers efficiently.<br/>• <b>Look at two bits (Q_0, Q_{-1}):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>00:</b> Do nothing (only arithmetic shift right A, Q, Q_{-1}).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>01:</b> End of a string of 1s ⇒ <b>Add Multiplicand (A = A + M)</b>, then shift right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>10:</b> Beginning of a string of 1s ⇒ <b>Subtract Multiplicand (A = A - M)</b>, then shift right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>11:</b> Do nothing (only arithmetic shift right).<br/>• <b>Best Case:</b> Alternating 0s or all 1s/all 0s (minimum additions/subtractions).<br/>• <b>Worst Case:</b> Isolated 1s (e.g. 01010101): requires n additions/subtractions.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Booth's algorithm, the shift operation is always an ARITHMETIC SHIFT RIGHT (ASR), which preserves the sign bit (MSB is replicated)!"}, {'number': '10.2', 'title': 'Hardwired vs Microprogrammed Control Unit', 'content': '• <b>Hardwired Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Implemented using combinational logic gates, decoders, and flip-flops.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Extremely fast execution</b>, but inflexible (cannot modify instructions without redesigning hardware). Used in RISC.<br/>• <b>Microprogrammed Control Unit:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Control signals generated by reading micro-instructions from Control Memory (ROM).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Flexible and easy to update, but slower due to memory access time. Used in CISC.<br/>• <b>Horizontal vs Vertical Microprogramming:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Horizontal:</b> 1 bit per control signal (no decoding, very wide, very fast).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Vertical:</b> Control signals encoded into groups (narrower, requires decoders, slower).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If a control unit has k mutually exclusive control signals, Horizontal needs k bits, whereas Vertical needs only ⌈log2(k + 1)⌉ bits!'}, {'number': '10.3', 'title': 'DMA (Direct Memory Access) & Cycle Stealing', 'content': '• <b>What is DMA?</b> Allows high-speed I/O devices (disk, network) to transfer blocks of data directly to/from memory without CPU intervention.<br/>• <b>Transfer Modes:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Burst Mode:</b> DMA holds bus until entire block is transferred. CPU is paused.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Cycle Stealing Mode:</b> DMA transfers 1 word per bus cycle, stealing cycles when CPU does not need system bus.<br/>• <b>CPU Paused Percentage in Cycle Stealing:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>% CPU Slowdown = (DMA Time per second / Total Time) × 100%</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;= (Device Data Rate / Bus Bandwidth) × 100%.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Cycle Stealing: Percentage of CPU time consumed = (Data preparation time per byte / Bus cycle time)^(-1) = (Device transfer rate / Bus bandwidth) × 100%.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: DMA Cycle Stealing Percentage', 'problem': 'A hard disk transfers data at 2 MB/sec. The system bus has a transfer speed of 10 MB/sec. What percentage of CPU cycle time is stolen by the DMA controller during transfer?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify Device transfer rate = 2 MB/sec.', 'Step 2: Identify Bus bandwidth = 10 MB/sec.', 'Step 3: Formula: CPU Time Stolen = (Device Rate / Bus Rate) × 100%.', 'Step 4: Compute: (2 MB/s / 10 MB/s) × 100% = 0.2 × 100% = 20%.'], 'answer': 'CPU cycle time stolen = 20%.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not confuse horizontal microprogramming (unencoded, 1 bit per signal) with vertical (encoded into log2 bits)!', 'In DMA questions, verify units: bytes/sec vs words/sec and bits vs bytes.']}, ('COA', 2): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Memory Hierarchy, Cache Mapping & AMAT', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Desk, Bookshelf & City Library', 'text': "• Memory Hierarchy: CPU Registers are the textbook open on your desk right now (instant access). Cache is the shelf within arm's reach (few seconds). RAM is the university library across the street (minutes). Hard disk is ordering a book from another country (days)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '11.1', 'title': 'Cache Address Splitting in Mapping Techniques', 'content': '• A main memory physical address is partitioned into 3 fields:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Direct Mapped Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Line / Block Bits | Word / Byte Offset ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Word Offset = <b>log2(Block Size in bytes)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Line Bits = <b>log2(Number of Cache Lines)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Tag Bits = Address Bits - Line Bits - Word Offset.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>k-Way Set-Associative Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Set Bits | Word / Byte Offset ]<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of Sets = <b>(Total Cache Lines) / k</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Set Bits = <b>log2(Number of Sets)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Fully Associative Cache:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- [ Tag Bits | Word / Byte Offset ] (Zero set/line bits! A block can go anywhere).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In k-way set-associative cache: Number of Sets = Total Cache Size / (k × Block Size). Tag Directory Size = (Number of Lines) × (Tag Bits + Valid Bit + Dirty Bit)!'}, {'number': '11.2', 'title': 'Average Memory Access Time (AMAT)', 'content': '• <b>Hierarchical Organization (CPU checks L1 first; on miss, visits L2/Memory):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = t_{hit_L1} + (Miss_Rate_L1 × Miss_Penalty_L1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If L2 is present: Miss_Penalty_L1 = t_{hit_L2} + (Miss_Rate_L2 × t_{memory}).<br/>• <b>Simultaneous Access (CPU searches L1 and Main Memory in parallel):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>AMAT = (Hit_Rate × t_{cache}) + (Miss_Rate × t_{memory})</b>.<br/>• <b>Write Policies:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Write-Through:</b> Updates both cache and memory immediately (slow, needs write buffer).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Write-Back:</b> Updates only cache; updates memory only when dirty block is evicted (faster, needs Dirty Bit).', 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unless the question explicitly specifies 'simultaneous access', ALWAYS use the Hierarchical formula where Miss Penalty includes access to the lower level!"}, {'number': '11.3', 'title': 'The 3 Cs of Cache Misses', 'content': '• <b>1. Compulsory (Cold) Miss:</b> First-ever access to a memory block. Unavoidable unless prefetching is used.<br/>• <b>2. Capacity Miss:</b> Cache is completely full and cannot hold all working-set blocks. Solved by increasing cache size.<br/>• <b>3. Conflict Miss:</b> Multiple blocks map to the same set/line even though other sets are empty. Occurs in Direct-Mapped and Set-Associative caches.<br/>• <b>Zero Conflict Misses:</b> Fully Associative cache has ZERO conflict misses!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fully Associative caches eliminate Conflict misses completely! They only suffer from Compulsory and Capacity misses.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Tag, Set, and Offset Bits', 'problem': 'A 32-bit byte-addressable system has a 64 KB 4-way set-associative cache with 32-byte blocks. Find the number of Tag, Set, and Word Offset bits.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Word Offset = log2(Block Size) = log2(32 bytes) = 5 bits.', 'Step 2: Total number of cache lines = Cache Size / Block Size = 64 KB / 32 B = 65536 / 32 = 2048 lines.', 'Step 3: Number of sets = Total Lines / k = 2048 / 4 = 512 sets.', 'Step 4: Set bits = log2(512) = 9 bits.', 'Step 5: Tag bits = 32 - (Set bits + Offset bits) = 32 - (9 + 5) = 32 - 14 = 18 bits.'], 'answer': 'Tag = 18 bits, Set = 9 bits, Word Offset = 5 bits.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Watch out for byte-addressable vs word-addressable systems! If word size is 4 bytes, offset uses word count instead of byte count.', 'Total cache size is data size only; tag directory overhead is NOT counted unless specifically requested.']}, ('COA', 3): {'sub_id': 'COA', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Pipeline Hazards, Speedup & Instruction Level Parallelism', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Laundromat Assembly Line', 'text': '• Laundry Pipelining: Person 1 washes, Person 2 dries, Person 3 folds, Person 4 stores. Instead of waiting 2 hours per load, a new load completes every 30 minutes! Pipelining increases throughput (instructions per second), NOT the latency of a single instruction.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '12.1', 'title': 'The Three Classic Pipeline Hazards', 'content': '• <b>1. Structural Hazard (Resource Conflict):</b> Hardware cannot support all concurrent instructions (e.g. single memory port accessed by IF and MEM simultaneously). Solved by separate Instruction and Data caches (Harvard Architecture).<br/>• <b>2. Data Hazard (Dependencies between instructions):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>RAW (Read After Write - True Dependency):</b> Instruction tries to read a register before prior instruction writes to it. Solved by <b>Operand Forwarding (Bypassing)</b> or compiler stalls.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>WAR (Write After Read - Anti-dependency):</b> Cannot happen in classic in-order RISC pipeline.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>WAW (Write After Write - Output dependency):</b> Cannot happen in in-order pipelines.<br/>• <b>3. Control Hazard (Branch Instruction):</b> Pipeline fetches wrong instructions before branch condition and target address are resolved. Solved by Branch Prediction, Delayed Branching, or target buffers.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a standard 5-stage in-order RISC pipeline (IF, ID, EX, MEM, WB), only RAW hazards cause data stalls! WAR and WAW hazards only occur in out-of-order execution processors.'}, {'number': '12.2', 'title': 'Pipeline Clock Period, Speedup & CPI', 'content': '• <b>Pipeline Clock Cycle (τ):</b> Set by the slowest stage plus register delay:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>τ = max(stage delays) + d</b>.<br/>• <b>Time to execute n instructions in a k-stage pipeline:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>T_pipe = [ k + (n - 1) + Stalls ] × τ</b>.<br/>• <b>Speedup (S) of Pipeline over Non-pipelined:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>S = T_{non-pipe} / T_{pipe} = (n × k × τ) / [ (k + n - 1) × τ ]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;For large n (n → ∞) and ideal CPI = 1: <b>Maximum Speedup = k</b> (number of stages!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Ideal CPI of an un-stalled pipeline is 1. If branch stalls or data hazards occur: Effective CPI = 1 + (Stall cycles per instruction). Actual Speedup = k / Effective CPI!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Pipeline Speedup with Hazards', 'problem': 'A 5-stage pipeline has stage delays of 150 ps, 120 ps, 180 ps, 160 ps, 140 ps with a pipeline register delay of 20 ps. In a non-pipelined implementation, total execution time per instruction is 750 ps. Calculate the clock period and maximum speedup for a large number of instructions.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Clock period τ = max(stage delays) + register delay = 180 ps + 20 ps = 200 ps.', 'Step 2: Non-pipelined time per instruction = 750 ps.', 'Step 3: Ideal pipelined time per instruction (n → ∞) = 1 instruction per clock cycle = 200 ps.', 'Step 4: Speedup S = 750 ps / 200 ps = 3.75.'], 'answer': 'Clock period = 200 ps; Speedup = 3.75.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not forget to add the pipeline register delay to the maximum stage delay!', 'Speedup is k only when all stages are perfectly balanced and register delay is zero.']}, ('PDS', 0): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Programming in C & Recursion Tracing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Russian Matryoshka Doll (Recursion)', 'text': '• Recursion: Opening a Russian nesting doll to find a smaller doll inside. You keep unpacking until you hit the smallest solid doll (Base Case). Then you pack them all back together (Call Stack unwinding)!\n• Array Decay: Handing someone a business card with your home address instead of shipping your entire house to them.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '13.1', 'title': 'Pointers & Array Equivalence in C', 'content': '• <b>What is an array name?</b> In C, an array name <code>arr</code> is a pointer to its first element: <code>arr == &arr[0]</code>.<br/>• <b>Subscript Rule:</b> <code>arr[i] == *(arr + i) == *(i + arr) == i[arr]</code>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In C, <code>3[arr]</code> is completely valid and equals <code>arr[3]</code>!<br/>• <b>Pointer Increment Nuances:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>*p++</code>: Read <code>*p</code> first, then increment pointer <code>p</code> to next memory slot.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>(*p)++</code>: Read value, increment the integer value inside memory by 1; pointer stays at same spot.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <code>*++p</code>: Increment pointer <code>p</code> first, then dereference.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: When an array is passed to a function, it decays to a simple pointer! Inside the function, sizeof(arr) gives the size of a pointer (4 or 8 bytes), NOT the full array size!'}, {'number': '13.2', 'title': 'Short-Circuit Evaluation in C', 'content': '• <b>Logical AND (expr1 && expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If expr1 evaluates to <b>0 (False)</b>, expr2 is <b>NEVER EXECUTED</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any side-effects (like <code>x++</code> or function calls) inside expr2 are completely skipped.<br/>• <b>Logical OR (expr1 || expr2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If expr1 evaluates to <b>non-zero (True)</b>, expr2 is <b>NEVER EXECUTED</b>!<br/>• <b>Precedence:</b> Logical AND (&&) has higher precedence than Logical OR (||).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In expression (a++ || b++): if a starts non-zero (True), b++ is NEVER evaluated, and b remains completely unchanged!'}, {'number': '13.3', 'title': 'Storage Classes & Scope in C', 'content': '• <b>auto:</b> Default for local variables. Stored in Stack; scope is block-local; lifetime is function call.<br/>• <b>register:</b> Request to store in CPU register for speed. Cannot take its address with <code>&var</code>!<br/>• <b>static:</b> Stored in Data Segment (BSS/Data). Retains its value across multiple function calls; initialized only once to 0.<br/>• <b>extern:</b> Declares a global variable defined in another file/scope without allocating new memory.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Static local variables are initialized ONLY ONCE when the program starts. On subsequent calls to the function, the initialization line is completely ignored!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Tracing Pointer Expressions in C', 'problem': "Given int a[] = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}; int *p = a; What is the output of printf('%d %d', *p++, (*p)++); followed by printf('%d', *p); ?", 'steps': ['Step 1: Pointer p points to a[0] (value 10).', "Step 2: Note that in C, argument evaluation order in printf is unspecified, but let's trace step by step sequentially:", 'Step 3: *p++ fetches 10, then advances p to point to a[1] (20).', 'Step 4: (*p)++ increments the value at a[1] from 20 to 21, but expression evaluates to 20.', 'Step 5: Afterwards, a[1] is now 21, and *p points to a[1] with value 21.'], 'answer': 'a[1] becomes 21; value pointed to by p is 21.'}], 'exam_traps': ["sizeof('a') in C is sizeof(int) = 4 bytes! In C++, sizeof('a') is 1 byte.", 'Array out-of-bounds access in C produces Undefined Behavior, not a compilation error or runtime crash!']}, ('PDS', 1): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Linear Data Structures - Stacks, Queues & Linked Lists', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Browser Back Button & Cafeteria Line', 'text': "• Stack (LIFO): The 'Back' button in your web browser. The last page you visited is the first page that pops back up.\n• Queue (FIFO): Standing in line at a cinema ticket counter. First person in line gets the ticket first."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '14.1', 'title': 'Infix, Postfix & Stack Conversions', 'content': '• <b>Postfix Evaluation using Stack:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Scan expression from left to right.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If operand (number), push onto stack.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If operator: pop two operands (op2 = pop(), op1 = pop()).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Compute: <b>result = op1 (operator) op2</b>. Push result back onto stack.<br/>• <b>Stack Permutations:</b> Number of valid stack permutations of n elements = <b>Catalan Number C_n = (1 / (n + 1)) × C(2n, n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Values: C_1 = 1, C_2 = 2, C_3 = 5, C_4 = 14, C_5 = 42.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In postfix evaluation, when an operator arrives: the first popped item is the RIGHT operand (op2), and the second popped item is the LEFT operand (op1): result = op1 op op2!'}, {'number': '14.2', 'title': 'Circular Queue Boundary Conditions', 'content': '• Using an array of size <b>N</b> with front and rear pointers:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Enqueue:</b> <code>rear = (rear + 1) % N; queue[rear] = item;</code><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dequeue:</b> <code>front = (front + 1) % N; item = queue[front];</code><br/>• <b>Full Condition (leaving 1 empty slot):</b> <b>(rear + 1) % N == front</b>.<br/>• <b>Empty Condition:</b> <b>front == rear</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of elements currently in Queue:</b> <b>(rear - front + N) % N</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a circular queue of array size N, leaving one empty slot allows max capacity of (N - 1) elements to distinguish Full from Empty!'}, {'number': '14.3', 'title': "Linked Lists & Floyd's Cycle Detection", 'content': "• <b>Floyd's Tortoise and Hare Algorithm:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Slow pointer moves 1 step; Fast pointer moves 2 steps.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If they meet, a <b>cycle exists</b> in the linked list!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Finding Start of Cycle:</b> Reset slow to Head. Keep fast at meeting point. Move both 1 step at a time. The node where they meet is the exact cycle entry node!<br/>• <b>Reversing a Singly Linked List:</b> Done in O(N) time and O(1) auxiliary space using 3 pointers (prev, curr, next).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Floyd's cycle detection runs in O(N) time with O(1) extra space! The distance from Head to cycle-start equals distance from meeting-point to cycle-start."}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Evaluating Postfix Expression', 'problem': 'Evaluate the postfix expression: 6 2 3 + * 3 8 2 / + * 2 ^ 3 +', 'steps': ['Step 1: Push 6, push 2, push 3. Stack: [6, 2, 3].', "Step 2: '+' arrives: pop 3 and 2. Compute 2 + 3 = 5. Push 5. Stack: [6, 5].", "Step 3: '*' arrives: pop 5 and 6. Compute 6 * 5 = 30. Push 30. Stack: [30].", 'Step 4: Push 3, push 8, push 2. Stack: [30, 3, 8, 2].', "Step 5: '/' arrives: pop 2 and 8. Compute 8 / 2 = 4. Push 4. Stack: [30, 3, 4].", "Step 6: '+' arrives: pop 4 and 3. Compute 3 + 4 = 7. Push 7. Stack: [30, 7].", "Step 7: '*' arrives: pop 7 and 30. Compute 30 * 7 = 210. Push 210. Stack: [210]."], 'answer': 'Evaluated result = 210 (before remaining operations).'}], 'exam_traps': ['In postfix to infix, parentheses must be preserved when operator precedence demands it!', 'Double-ended queue (Deque) can implement both a Stack and a Queue in O(1) time per operation.']}, ('PDS', 2): {'sub_id': 'PDS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Trees, Binary Search Trees & Binary Heaps', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Corporate Hierarchy & Hospital Emergency Triage', 'text': '• Binary Tree: Like a CEO who has at most 2 direct Vice Presidents, each VP having at most 2 directors.\n• Heap (Priority Queue): Like an emergency room triage where patients are treated by severity of injury (highest priority first), regardless of arrival time!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '15.1', 'title': 'Binary Tree Fundamental Properties', 'content': '• <b>Key Formulas for Binary Trees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Leaf vs Degree-2 Nodes:</b> In any binary tree: <b>n_0 = n_2 + 1</b> (Number of leaf nodes = Number of degree-2 nodes + 1).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Max nodes at height h (root at h=0):</b> <b>2^(h + 1) - 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Min height for n nodes:</b> <b>⌈log2(n + 1)⌉ - 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Unique Tree Reconstruction:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Inorder + Preorder gives a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Inorder + Postorder gives a UNIQUE binary tree.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Preorder + Postorder does NOT give a unique tree (unless strictly full binary tree)!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Inorder traversal MUST be given to uniquely reconstruct a binary tree! Preorder + Postorder is NEVER sufficient for general binary trees.'}, {'number': '15.2', 'title': 'Binary Search Trees (BST) & AVL Trees', 'content': '• <b>BST Inorder Traversal:</b> Inorder traversal of any BST always produces strictly ASCENDING sorted order.<br/>• <b>Number of distinct BSTs with n distinct keys:</b> <b>Catalan Number C_n</b>.<br/>• <b>AVL Trees (Height-Balanced BST):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Balance Factor = <b>Height(Left Subtree) - Height(Right Subtree) ∈ {-1, 0, +1}</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- 4 Rebalancing Rotations: LL, RR (single rotations) and LR, RL (double rotations).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum nodes in AVL of height h: <b>N(h) = N(h - 1) + N(h - 2) + 1</b> (with N(0)=1, N(1)=2).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In any BST, the node with maximum value has NO right child, and the node with minimum value has NO left child!'}, {'number': '15.3', 'title': 'Binary Heap Array Representation & Complexities', 'content': '• In a 1-based index array representation for a node at index <b>i</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parent index: <b>⌊i / 2⌋</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Left child index: <b>2i</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Right child index: <b>2i + 1</b>.<br/>• <b>Heap Operations Time Complexity:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Find Min/Max: <b>O(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Insert: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Delete Min/Max: <b>O(log n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Build-Heap (from arbitrary array):</b> <b>O(n)</b> time (NOT O(n log n)! Tested repeatedly in GATE!).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Building a heap of n elements takes O(n) time, NOT O(n log n)! Sum of heights ∑ (h / 2^h) converges to 2, proving linear time.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Reconstructing Tree from Preorder & Inorder', 'problem': 'Given Preorder: [A, B, D, E, C, F] and Inorder: [D, B, E, A, F, C]. Find the Postorder traversal.', 'steps': ['Step 1: First element in Preorder is Root = A.', 'Step 2: Locate A in Inorder: Left subtree is {D, B, E}, Right subtree is {F, C}.', 'Step 3: In Preorder, next 3 elements {B, D, E} belong to Left subtree. Root of Left subtree is B.', 'Step 4: Locate B in Inorder: D is Left child of B, E is Right child of B.', 'Step 5: For Right subtree: Preorder has C first ⇒ C is root; Inorder has F before C ⇒ F is Left child of C.', 'Step 6: Compute Postorder (Left, Right, Root): D, E, B, F, C, A.'], 'answer': 'Postorder: D, E, B, F, C, A.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In a Min-Heap, the maximum element is always at one of the leaf nodes (indices ⌊n/2⌋ + 1 to n)!', 'Deleting an arbitrary node from a heap takes O(log n) time, provided its index is known.']}, ('ALGO', 0): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Asymptotic Analysis, Master Theorem & Recurrences', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Speed of Transport Modes', 'text': "• Big-O: An upper bound promise. Like saying 'a flight from Delhi to Mumbai takes at most 3 hours'. It might take 2 hours, but will never exceed 3 hours in the worst case.\n• Master Theorem: A standard cookbook recipe for dividing a giant task into smaller sub-tasks."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '16.1', 'title': 'Big-O, Big-Omega & Big-Theta Explained', 'content': '• <b>Big-O (O):</b> Upper Bound: f(n) ≤ c · g(n) for all n ≥ n_0.<br/>• <b>Big-Omega (Ω):</b> Lower Bound: f(n) ≥ c · g(n) for all n ≥ n_0.<br/>• <b>Big-Theta (Θ):</b> Tight Bound: c1·g(n) ≤ f(n) ≤ c2·g(n). True if and only if both O and Ω hold!<br/>• <b>Standard Rate of Growth Hierarchy:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>c &lt; log(log n) &lt; log n &lt; √n &lt; n &lt; n log n &lt; n² &lt; n³ &lt; 2^n &lt; n! &lt; n^n</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: f(n) = Θ(g(n)) IF AND ONLY IF f(n) = O(g(n)) AND f(n) = Ω(g(n)). If one fails, Θ cannot hold!'}, {'number': '16.2', 'title': 'Master Theorem for Divide and Conquer', 'content': '• For recurrence: <b>T(n) = a · T(n / b) + Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b> (with a ≥ 1, b > 1):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Compare <b>a</b> with <b>b^k</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Case 1 (a > b^k):</b> <b>T(n) = Θ(n^(log_b a))</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Case 2 (a = b^k):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p > -1: <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;• If p = -1: T(n) = Θ(n^k · log(log n)).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Case 3 (a < b^k):</b> <b>T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^p n)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Master theorem CANNOT be applied if: (1) a is not constant (e.g. 2^n T(n/2)), (2) b is not constant, or (3) f(n) is not a polynomial (e.g. T(n) = 2T(n/2) + n!).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Solving Recurrence with Master Theorem', 'problem': 'Solve the recurrence relation T(n) = 4 T(n / 2) + n².', 'steps': ['Step 1: Identify parameters: a = 4, b = 2, f(n) = n² ⇒ k = 2, p = 0.', 'Step 2: Calculate b^k = 2² = 4.', 'Step 3: Compare a with b^k: a = 4 and b^k = 4 ⇒ a == b^k (Case 2).', 'Step 4: Since p = 0 > -1, formula gives T(n) = Θ(n^k · log^{p+1} n) = Θ(n² · log n).'], 'answer': 'T(n) = Θ(n² log n).'}], 'exam_traps': ['In T(n) = 2 T(n/2) + n/log n: Master Theorem fails because f(n) is not polynomially larger/smaller!', 'Master theorem for subtracting recurrences: T(n) = T(n - 1) + n gives Θ(n²).']}, ('ALGO', 1): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Sorting Algorithms, Lower Bounds & Hash Tables', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Organizing Playing Cards & Coat Check Tickets', 'text': '• Insertion Sort: Like sorting playing cards in your hand—you pick each new card and slide it into its correct position among already sorted cards.\n• Hash Table: Like a coat checkroom with 100 hooks. You get a numbered ticket that instantly maps to your exact hook without searching the whole room.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '17.1', 'title': 'Master Summary of Sorting Algorithms', 'content': '• <b>Comparison Table for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Insertion Sort:</b> Best: O(n), Avg: O(n²), Worst: O(n²), Space: O(1), <b>Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Merge Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n), Avg: O(n log n), Worst: O(n log n), Space: O(n), <b>Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Heap Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n), Avg: O(n log n), Worst: O(n log n), Space: O(1), <b>NOT Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Quick Sort:</b> Best: O(n log n), Avg: O(n log n), Worst: O(n²), Space: O(log n), <b>NOT Stable</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Counting Sort:</b> O(n + k) time and O(k) space (non-comparison, Stable).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merge Sort and Insertion Sort are STABLE! Quick Sort and Heap Sort are UNSTABLE! The theoretical lower bound for any comparison-based sort is Ω(n log n).'}, {'number': '17.2', 'title': 'Hash Table Collisions: Chaining vs Open Addressing', 'content': "• <b>Separate Chaining:</b> Each slot points to a linked list. Load factor α can exceed 1.<br/>• <b>Open Addressing:</b> All elements stored inside the hash table array. Load factor α ≤ 1.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Linear Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + i) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Primary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Quadratic Probing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h'(k) + c1·i + c2·i²) % m</code>. Suffers from <b>Secondary Clustering</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Double Hashing:</b> <code>h(k, i) = (h1(k) + i · h2(k)) % m</code>. Eliminates clustering (h2(k) must be coprime to m).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In open addressing, successful search takes O((1/α) ln(1/(1-α))) and unsuccessful search takes O(1 / (1 - α)) expected probes!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Linear Probing Hash Collisions', 'problem': 'Insert keys [12, 22, 32, 42] into a hash table of size 10 using hash function h(k) = k % 10 with Linear Probing.', 'steps': ['Step 1: 12 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Slot 2 is empty. Store 12 at index 2.', 'Step 2: 22 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Collision! Probe (2 + 1) % 10 = 3. Slot 3 is empty. Store 22 at index 3.', 'Step 3: 32 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Collision at 2, collision at 3! Probe (2 + 2) % 10 = 4. Slot 4 is empty. Store 32 at index 4.', 'Step 4: 42 % 10 = 2 ⇒ Collisions at 2, 3, 4! Probe (2 + 3) % 10 = 5. Slot 5 is empty. Store 42 at index 5.'], 'answer': 'Final positions: 12 at 2, 22 at 3, 32 at 4, 42 at 5.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In QuickSort, picking the first or last element as pivot on an ALREADY SORTED array results in WORST-CASE O(n²) time!', 'Randomized QuickSort avoids worst-case for sorted inputs with high probability, but theoretical worst case is still O(n²).']}, ('ALGO', 2): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Greedy Algorithms, Dynamic Programming & Divide-and-Conquer', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Cashier Change & Remembering Past Math Results', 'text': "• Greedy: Like a cashier giving you change using the fewest coins by always picking the largest coin denomination first (25c, then 10c, etc.).\n• Dynamic Programming: '1 + 1 + 1 + 1 = 4'. If I write another '+ 1', you instantly say '5' because you REMEMBERED the previous 4 without recalculating from scratch!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '18.1', 'title': 'Greedy Method vs Dynamic Programming', 'content': "• <b>Greedy Method:</b> Makes locally optimal choice at each step hoping for global optimum. Never backtracks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: Fractional Knapsack, Huffman Coding, Kruskal's, Prim's, Dijkstra.<br/>• <b>Dynamic Programming (DP):</b> Solves subproblems once, stores results in a table (memoization/tabulation).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Requires <b>Optimal Substructure</b> AND <b>Overlapping Subproblems</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Examples: 0/1 Knapsack, Longest Common Subsequence (LCS), Matrix Chain Multiplication, Floyd-Warshall.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fractional Knapsack is solved by Greedy (sort by value/weight in O(n log n)). 0/1 Knapsack CANNOT be solved by Greedy; it requires Dynamic Programming in O(n × W) pseudo-polynomial time!'}, {'number': '18.2', 'title': 'Longest Common Subsequence (LCS) Recurrence', 'content': '• For strings X of length m and Y of length n:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] == Y[j]</b>: <b>LCS[i, j] = 1 + LCS[i - 1, j - 1]</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• If <b>X[i] ≠ Y[j]</b>: <b>LCS[i, j] = max(LCS[i - 1, j], LCS[i, j - 1])</b>.<br/>• <b>Time & Space:</b> O(m × n) time, reducible to O(min(m, n)) space using two rows.<br/>• <b>Matrix Chain Multiplication:</b> Cost recurrence: <b>m[i, j] = min_{i ≤ k < j} { m[i, k] + m[k+1, j] + p_{i-1}·p_k·p_j }</b>. Time: O(n³).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Matrix Chain Multiplication with n matrices, number of different parenthesizations is given by Catalan Number C_{n-1}!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Huffman Code Tree Construction', 'problem': 'Given frequencies: a: 5, b: 9, c: 12, d: 13, e: 16, f: 45. Find the length of Huffman encoded string.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Pick two smallest: 5 and 9 ⇒ merge to node 14. Remaining: [c:12, d:13, (5+9):14, e:16, f:45].', 'Step 2: Pick two smallest: 12 and 13 ⇒ merge to node 25. Remaining: [14, e:16, 25, f:45].', 'Step 3: Pick two smallest: 14 and 16 ⇒ merge to node 30. Remaining: [25, 30, f:45].', 'Step 4: Pick two smallest: 25 and 30 ⇒ merge to node 55. Remaining: [f:45, 55].', 'Step 5: Merge 45 and 55 ⇒ root 100.', 'Step 6: Code lengths: f=1 bit, c=3, d=3, e=3, a=4, b=4. Weighted path length = (45×1) + (12×3) + (13×3) + (16×3) + (5×4) + (9×4) = 45 + 36 + 39 + 48 + 20 + 36 = 224 bits.'], 'answer': 'Total encoded length = 224 bits.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Huffman code is a prefix code: NO valid codeword is a prefix of any other codeword!', 'Huffman tree is always a strictly Full Binary Tree (every internal node has exactly 2 children).']}, ('ALGO', 3): {'sub_id': 'ALGO', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'Graph Traversals, MST & Shortest Paths', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: GPS Navigation & Google Maps', 'text': "• Dijkstra's Algorithm: How Google Maps finds the quickest driving route to your destination without negative road tolls.\n• Minimum Spanning Tree: Laying down fiber-optic internet cables between cities with the absolute minimum total cable length while ensuring every city is connected."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '19.1', 'title': 'BFS vs DFS Comparison & Applications', 'content': "• <b>Breadth-First Search (BFS):</b> Uses a <b>Queue</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds the <b>shortest path in unweighted graphs</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects bipartiteness (2-coloring).<br/>• <b>Depth-First Search (DFS):</b> Uses a <b>Stack / Recursion</b>. Time: <b>O(V + E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects cycles in directed graphs (via <b>Back Edges</b>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computes <b>Topological Sort</b> of a DAG (reverse of finish times).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Finds Strongly Connected Components (Kosaraju's / Tarjan's algorithm).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In DFS of a directed graph, a cycle exists IF AND ONLY IF there is at least one BACK EDGE! Forward and Cross edges do NOT indicate a cycle.'}, {'number': '19.2', 'title': 'Dijkstra vs Bellman-Ford vs Floyd-Warshall', 'content': "• <b>Dijkstra's Algorithm:</b> Single-Source Shortest Path for <b>non-negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Time: <b>O((V + E) log V)</b> using min-heap. FAILS for negative edge weights!<br/>• <b>Bellman-Ford Algorithm:</b> Works with <b>negative edge weights</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relaxes all edges (V - 1) times. Time: <b>O(V × E)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Detects negative-weight cycles (if distance decreases on V-th relaxation).<br/>• <b>Floyd-Warshall Algorithm:</b> All-Pairs Shortest Path in <b>O(V³)</b> time using dynamic programming.<br/>• <b>Kruskal vs Prim (MST):</b> Kruskal uses edges (O(E log E) with DSU); Prim grows from a vertex (O(E + V log V)).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If all edge weights in a graph are distinct, the Minimum Spanning Tree (MST) is 100% UNIQUE! Shortest path tree is NOT necessarily unique.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Kruskal's MST Edge Selection", 'problem': 'Given edges sorted by weight: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (A-C, 2), (C-D, 3), (B-D, 4). Which edges are included in the MST?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Pick (A-B, 1) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 2: Pick (B-C, 2) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 3: Consider (A-C, 2) ⇒ A, B, C are already connected! Adding A-C creates cycle A-B-C-A. REJECT.', 'Step 4: Pick (C-D, 3) ⇒ No cycle, add to MST.', 'Step 5: We have 4 vertices and 3 edges (V - 1 = 3). MST is complete!'], 'answer': 'MST edges: (A-B, 1), (B-C, 2), (C-D, 3). Total weight = 6.'}], 'exam_traps': ["Dijkstra's algorithm can enter an infinite loop or give wrong answers if negative edge weights are present!", 'Topological sort exists IF AND ONLY IF the graph is a Directed Acyclic Graph (DAG).']}, ('TOC', 0): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Finite Automata, Regular Expressions & Minimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Vending Machine States & Traffic Light Controller', 'text': "• Finite Automata: Like a soda vending machine. Inserting a coin moves it from state 'Waiting' to 'Paid'. Pressing button delivers soda and returns to 'Waiting'. The machine has finite memory—it only remembers its current state!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '20.1', 'title': 'DFA vs NFA Equivalences & Power', 'content': '• <b>Equivalent Power:</b> Every NFA can be converted into an equivalent DFA using <b>Subset Construction</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If an NFA has <b>n states</b>, the equivalent minimized DFA has at most <b>2^n states</b>.<br/>• <b>Key Distinctions:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. In DFA: For every state and input symbol, there is <b>exactly ONE</b> transition: δ: Q × Σ → Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. In NFA: There can be 0, 1, or multiple transitions, plus ε-transitions: δ: Q × (Σ ∪ {ε}) → 2^Q.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Dead States:</b> DFA requires explicit trap/dead states for invalid inputs; NFA simply drops the transition.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: DFA, NFA, and ε-NFA all have EQUAL computational power! They all recognize the exact same family of languages: Regular Languages.'}, {'number': '20.2', 'title': "Regular Expressions & Arden's Theorem", 'content': "• <b>Arden's Theorem:</b> If P and Q are regular expressions over Σ and P does not contain ε, then equation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>R = Q + RP</b> has a <b>UNIQUE solution: R = QP*</b>.<br/>• <b>Identities to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• (r*)* = r*<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• (r + s)* = (r* · s*)* = (r* + s*)*<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• r(s · r)* = (r · s)* · r<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• ε + r · r* = r* = ε + r* · r", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Arden's theorem R = Q + RP gives the unique solution R = QP*. Notice P is on the right of R! If R = Q + PR (left recursion), the solution is P*Q."}, {'number': '20.3', 'title': 'Regular Language Closure Properties', 'content': "• <b>Regular Languages are CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Union:</b> L1 ∪ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L' is Regular (swap accept and non-accept states in DFA!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Difference:</b> L1 - L2 = L1 ∩ L2' is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Concatenation:</b> L1 · L2 is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Kleene Star:</b> L* is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reversal:</b> L^R is Regular.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Homomorphism & Inverse Homomorphism:</b> Regular.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regular languages are closed under ALL standard boolean and algebraic operations! To complement a DFA, swap final and non-final states (make sure it is a complete DFA first, including trap states)!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Minimum DFA States for String Ending in '01'", 'problem': "Find the minimum number of states in a DFA accepting all binary strings ending with substring '01'.", 'steps': ["Step 1: Length of pattern '01' is 2.", 'Step 2: For strings ending with a specific pattern of length k, minimum DFA states = k + 1.', 'Step 3: Here k = 2 ⇒ States = 2 + 1 = 3 states: q0 (start/empty), q1 (last bit 0), q2 (last bits 01 - Accept).', "Step 4: Trace transitions: from q2 on '0' go to q1; on '1' go to q0."], 'answer': 'Minimum states = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Swapping final and non-final states on an NFA does NOT give the complement language! You must convert NFA to DFA first.', 'Pumping Lemma can prove a language is NOT regular; it can NEVER prove a language IS regular.']}, ('TOC', 1): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Context-Free Grammars, Pushdown Automata & Chomsky Hierarchy', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Balancing Scale & Parenthesis Matcher', 'text': "• PDA Stack: Like a stack of plates in a cafeteria. To check if parentheses match: push '(' onto the stack; when ')' arrives, pop '(' off. If stack is empty at the end, parentheses were perfectly balanced!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '21.1', 'title': 'Deterministic PDA (DPDA) vs Non-Deterministic PDA (NPDA)', 'content': "• <b>Crucial Distinction:</b> NPDA is <b>strictly more powerful</b> than DPDA!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- DPDA recognizes <b>Deterministic Context-Free Languages (DCFL)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- NPDA recognizes <b>Context-Free Languages (CFL)</b>.<br/>• <b>Examples to Memorize for GATE:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. L = { w c w^R | w ∈ {0,1}* } has a marker 'c' ⇒ <b>DCFL</b> (accepted by DPDA).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. L = { w w^R | w ∈ {0,1}* } has NO marker (even palindrome) ⇒ <b>CFL, but NOT DCFL</b> (requires non-deterministic guess of midpoint!).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. L = { a^n b^n c^n } ⇒ <b>NOT CFL</b> (requires 2 stacks = Turing Machine).", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Unlike Finite Automata where DFA == NFA, in Pushdown Automata NPDA is STRICTLY MORE POWERFUL than DPDA: DCFL ⊂ CFL!'}, {'number': '21.2', 'title': 'CFL Closure Properties (The Famous Exceptions!)', 'content': "• <b>CFLs are CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Union:</b> L1 ∪ L2 is CFL.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Concatenation:</b> L1 · L2 is CFL.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Kleene Star:</b> L* is CFL.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Reversal:</b> L^R is CFL.<br/>• <b>CFLs are NOT CLOSED under:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Intersection:</b> L1 ∩ L2 is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Complement:</b> L' is NOT necessarily CFL!<br/>• <b>Intersection with Regular Language:</b> If L is CFL and R is Regular, then <b>L ∩ R is ALWAYS CFL</b>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: CFL is NOT closed under Intersection or Complement! But DCFL is CLOSED under Complement (though NOT closed under Union or Intersection).'}, {'number': '21.3', 'title': 'The Chomsky Hierarchy of Languages', 'content': '• <b>Type 3 (Regular):</b> Accepted by Finite Automata. Grammar: A → aB or A → a.<br/>• <b>Type 2 (Context-Free):</b> Accepted by Pushdown Automata. Grammar: A → α (single variable on LHS).<br/>• <b>Type 1 (Context-Sensitive):</b> Accepted by Linear Bounded Automata (LBA). Grammar: α → β where |α| ≤ |β|.<br/>• <b>Type 0 (Unrestricted / Recursively Enumerable):</b> Accepted by Turing Machine. Grammar: α → β.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strict containment: Regular ⊂ DCFL ⊂ CFL ⊂ CSL ⊂ Recursive ⊂ Recursively Enumerable!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Checking CFL Intersection', 'problem': 'Let L1 = { a^n b^n c^m | n, m ≥ 1 } and L2 = { a^m b^n c^n | m, n ≥ 1 }. Are L1 and L2 CFLs? Is L1 ∩ L2 a CFL?', 'steps': ["Step 1: L1 requires matching count of a's with b's (c's independent) ⇒ Can be done with 1 stack ⇒ L1 is CFL.", "Step 2: L2 requires matching count of b's with c's (a's independent) ⇒ Can be done with 1 stack ⇒ L2 is CFL.", 'Step 3: Intersection L1 ∩ L2 = { a^n b^n c^n | n ≥ 1 }.', 'Step 4: L1 ∩ L2 requires comparing 3 counts simultaneously, impossible with 1 stack.', 'Step 5: Therefore, L1 ∩ L2 is NOT a CFL (it is a Context-Sensitive Language).'], 'answer': 'L1 and L2 are CFLs, but their intersection L1 ∩ L2 is NOT a CFL.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Inherent ambiguity: Some CFLs are inherently ambiguous (every grammar for them is ambiguous, e.g. {a^i b^j c^k | i=j or j=k}).', 'DCFLs are NEVER ambiguous! Every DCFL has an unambiguous grammar.']}, ('TOC', 2): {'sub_id': 'TOC', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': "Turing Machines, Decidability & Rice's Theorem", 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Infinite Tape & Software Freeze', 'text': '• Halting Problem: Can anyone write an antivirus software that inspects any arbitrary code and guarantees whether it will eventually terminate or freeze in an infinite loop? Alan Turing proved it is mathematically IMPOSSIBLE for all programs!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '22.1', 'title': 'Recursive (REC) vs Recursively Enumerable (RE)', 'content': "• <b>Recursive Language (REC / Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Turing Machine halts on EVERY input (Halts and Accepts on w ∈ L; Halts and Rejects on w ∉ L).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Closed under ALL standard operations</b> including Complement: If L is REC, L' is REC.<br/>• <b>Recursively Enumerable Language (RE / Semi-Decidable):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Turing machine halts and accepts if w ∈ L.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If w ∉ L, it may reject OR run in an infinite loop forever!<br/>• <b>The Complement Duality Theorem:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>A language L is Recursive IF AND ONLY IF both L and its complement L' are Recursively Enumerable!</b>", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If L is RE and L' is also RE, then L is 100% DECIDABLE (Recursive)! If L is RE and undecidable, then L' CANNOT even be RE!"}, {'number': '22.2', 'title': "Rice's Theorem for Turing Machines", 'content': "• <b>Rice's Theorem (Part 1):</b> Any <b>non-trivial semantic (language) property</b> of the language accepted by a Turing Machine is <b>UNDECIDABLE</b>.<br/>• <b>What is a Semantic Property?</b> A property about the LANGUAGE recognized by the TM (e.g. 'Is L(M) empty?', 'Is L(M) finite?', 'Is L(M) regular?').<br/>• <b>Non-trivial means:</b> True for some TMs and False for other TMs.<br/>• <b>Syntactic Properties (Rice does NOT apply):</b> Questions about the machine structure itself (e.g. 'Does TM have 5 states?', 'Does TM take 10 steps on input w?') are DECIDABLE.", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In GATE, if a question asks 'Is property P decidable for a Turing Machine?' and P is about L(M) (like regularity, emptiness, finiteness, equivalence), the answer is INSTANTLY UNDECIDABLE by Rice's Theorem!"}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': "Worked Example: Applying Rice's Theorem", 'problem': "Determine if the following problem is decidable: 'Given a Turing Machine M, does M accept a language that contains at least one palindrome?'", 'steps': ["Step 1: Check if the property is semantic: 'Does L(M) contain a palindrome?' is a property of the language recognized by M (Semantic).", 'Step 2: Check if non-trivial: Some TMs accept palindromes (e.g. TM for (01)*), while others accept nothing (empty language). Non-trivial!', "Step 3: By Rice's Theorem Part 1, the problem is UNDECIDABLE."], 'answer': "Undecidable by Rice's Theorem."}], 'exam_traps': ["'Does TM M take at most 100 steps on input w?' is DECIDABLE because you can simulate it for 100 steps and stop!", 'Post Correspondence Problem (PCP) is undecidable for alphabet size ≥ 2, but decidable for unary alphabet (size 1).']}, ('CD', 0): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Lexical Analysis, FIRST & FOLLOW Sets', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Spell Checker & Grammar Dictionary', 'text': "• Lexer: Like checking a dictionary to see if letters form valid English words ('int', 'sum', '100').\n• FIRST set: Like checking what letter a sentence could possibly begin with.\n• FOLLOW set: Like asking what word can legally appear immediately to the right of a noun in a sentence."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '23.1', 'title': 'Rules for Calculating FIRST Sets', 'content': '• <b>Definition:</b> FIRST(X) is the set of terminal symbols that appear as the first symbol of any string derived from X.<br/>• <b>Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. If X is a terminal: <b>FIRST(X) = { X }</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. If X → ε is a production: Add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If X → Y1 Y2 ... Yk:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y1) to FIRST(X).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If Y1 derives ε, add all non-ε symbols from FIRST(Y2) to FIRST(X), and so forth.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- If ALL Y1 ... Yk derive ε, add <b>ε</b> to FIRST(X).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Terminal symbols ALWAYS have FIRST(a) = { a }. When calculating FIRST(Y1 Y2), do not stop at Y1 if Y1 can produce ε!'}, {'number': '23.2', 'title': 'Rules for Calculating FOLLOW Sets', 'content': '• <b>Definition:</b> FOLLOW(A) is the set of terminals that can appear immediately to the right of non-terminal A in some sentential form.<br/>• <b>Three Golden Computation Rules:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. For Start Symbol S: Add <b>$ (End-of-input marker)</b> to FOLLOW(S).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. For production A → α B β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FIRST(β) except ε</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. For production A → α B OR A → α B β (where FIRST(β) contains ε):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;- Everything in <b>FOLLOW(A)</b> is added to FOLLOW(B).<br/>• <b>FOLLOW sets NEVER contain ε!</b>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: FOLLOW set NEVER CONTAINS ε (epsilon)! If your calculation produces ε in a FOLLOW set, it is 100% WRONG!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Computing FIRST and FOLLOW Sets', 'problem': 'Given grammar: S → A B; A → a | ε; B → b | ε. Compute FIRST(S) and FOLLOW(A).', 'steps': ['Step 1: FIRST(A) = { a, ε }; FIRST(B) = { b, ε }.', 'Step 2: For S → A B: FIRST(S) includes FIRST(A) minus ε = { a }. Since A → ε, include FIRST(B) = { b }. Since both A and B can produce ε, add ε. ⇒ FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }.', 'Step 3: To find FOLLOW(A): Look at S → A B. Non-terminal B immediately follows A.', 'Step 4: Everything in FIRST(B) except ε goes into FOLLOW(A) ⇒ { b }.', 'Step 5: Since B derives ε, everything in FOLLOW(S) also goes into FOLLOW(A).', 'Step 6: S is start symbol ⇒ $ ∈ FOLLOW(S). Since S appears nowhere on RHS, FOLLOW(S) = { $ }.', 'Step 7: Therefore, FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.'], 'answer': 'FIRST(S) = { a, b, ε }; FOLLOW(A) = { b, $ }.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In token counting: String constants like "Hello, World!" count as ONE single token!', "Identifiers vs Keywords: In C lexing, keywords like 'int', 'return' are reserved and not identifiers."]}, ('CD', 1): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Top-Down (LL(1)) & Bottom-Up (LR) Parsing', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Predictive Text & Jigsaw Puzzle', 'text': '• LL(1) Parser: Like a predictive keyboard on your phone that looks ahead by 1 character to predict the next word without backtracking.\n• LR Parser: Like building a jigsaw puzzle from individual bottom pieces upward until the entire picture (start symbol) is assembled.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '24.1', 'title': 'LL(1) Grammar Conditions', 'content': '• A grammar is <b>LL(1)</b> if and only if for every non-terminal A with multiple productions A → α | β:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = ∅</b> (disjoint FIRST sets).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. At most one of α or β can derive ε.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. If β derives ε, then <b>FIRST(α) ∩ FOLLOW(A) = ∅</b>.<br/>• <b>Instant Disqualifiers for LL(1):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any grammar with <b>Left Recursion (A → A α)</b> is NEVER LL(1)!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any grammar with <b>Ambiguity</b> is NEVER LL(1)!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Left recursion (immediate or indirect) INSTANTLY disqualifies a grammar from being LL(1)! Eliminate left recursion and left factoring before building LL(1) table.'}, {'number': '24.2', 'title': 'Hierarchy and Power of LR Parsers', 'content': '• <b>Power Hierarchy (Strict Containment):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>LR(0) ⊂ SLR(1) ⊂ LALR(1) ⊂ CLR(1)</b>.<br/>• <b>Number of Parsing States:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>States in LR(0) == States in SLR(1) == States in LALR(1)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- CLR(1) has significantly MORE states (splits states with different lookaheads).<br/>• <b>Shift-Reduce (SR) and Reduce-Reduce (RR) Conflicts:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In LR(0): Reduce action placed in ALL columns.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- In SLR(1): Reduce action placed ONLY in <b>FOLLOW(A)</b> columns.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Merging states from CLR(1) to LALR(1) <b>NEVER produces Shift-Reduce conflicts</b>, but MAY produce Reduce-Reduce conflicts!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Merging CLR(1) states to form LALR(1) can NEVER introduce a new Shift-Reduce (SR) conflict! It can only introduce Reduce-Reduce (RR) conflicts.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Checking if Grammar is LL(1)', 'problem': 'Is grammar S → a S | a LL(1)?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Check productions for S: α = a S and β = a.', 'Step 2: FIRST(α) = FIRST(a S) = { a }.', 'Step 3: FIRST(β) = FIRST(a) = { a }.', 'Step 4: Check intersection: FIRST(α) ∩ FIRST(β) = { a } ∩ { a } = { a } ≠ ∅.', "Step 5: Since intersection is not empty, the parser cannot decide which rule to use when lookahead is 'a'."], 'answer': "Grammar is NOT LL(1) (requires left factoring to S → a S' with S' → S | ε)."}], 'exam_traps': ['Every SLR(1) grammar is an LALR(1) grammar, but not vice versa!', 'Every ambiguous grammar is neither LL(1) nor LR(k) for any k.']}, ('CD', 2): {'sub_id': 'CD', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'SDT, Intermediate Code & Optimization', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Inheritance Tree & Dead Code Cleanup', 'text': '• S-attributed: Like child elements calculating their own weights and reporting them UP to the parent node (Bottom-Up).\n• L-attributed: Like parents sharing an inheritance with children from left to right.\n• Dead Code: Removing unused furniture from a room so you never spend energy cleaning it!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '25.1', 'title': 'S-Attributed vs L-Attributed SDT', 'content': '• <b>S-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses ONLY <b>Synthesized Attributes</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Computed bottom-up in a parse tree from child values to parent: A.val = f(X1.val, ..., Xk.val).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can be evaluated during <b>LR parsing (bottom-up)</b> without modifying parse stack.<br/>• <b>L-Attributed Definition:</b> Uses Synthesized attributes AND restricted Inherited attributes.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- An inherited attribute of node X_j can depend only on:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Attributes of symbols to its <b>LEFT (X_1, X_2, ..., X_{j-1})</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Inherited attributes of the <b>parent symbol A</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Can be evaluated during <b>Depth-First Search (left-to-right)</b> traversal.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Every S-attributed SDT is automatically an L-attributed SDT! The reverse is NOT true: L-attributed includes inherited attributes that depend on left siblings.'}, {'number': '25.2', 'title': 'Common Code Optimizations & Basic Blocks', 'content': '• <b>Basic Block:</b> A sequence of consecutive statements with one entry point and one exit point.<br/>• <b>Leaders in 3AC:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Statement 1 is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Target of any conditional/unconditional jump (GOTO) is a leader.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Statement immediately following any jump is a leader.<br/>• <b>Optimization Types:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Folding:</b> Compute constant expressions at compile time (e.g. <code>x = 3 * 7</code> becomes <code>x = 21</code>).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Constant Propagation:</b> Replace variable with its known constant value.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Dead Code Elimination:</b> Remove code whose result is never used or statements that are never reached.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Strength Reduction:</b> Replace expensive operations with cheaper ones (e.g. <code>x * 2</code> replaced by <code>x << 1</code> or <code>x + x</code>).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Strength reduction replaces an expensive operation with a cheaper one (e.g. multiplication replaced by addition in loops). It does NOT eliminate code!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Identifying Leaders and Basic Blocks', 'problem': 'Given 3-Address Code: (1) i = 1; (2) j = 1; (3) t1 = 10 * i; (4) t2 = t1 + j; (5) if t2 < 100 goto (3); (6) goto (1). How many basic blocks are there?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Statement (1) is a leader (first statement).', 'Step 2: Target of goto (3) is statement (3) ⇒ (3) is a leader.', 'Step 3: Target of goto (1) is statement (1) (already a leader).', 'Step 4: Statement following jump (5) is statement (6) ⇒ (6) is a leader.', 'Step 5: Leaders are statements (1), (3), and (6).', 'Step 6: Basic blocks: Block 1: [(1), (2)]; Block 2: [(3), (4), (5)]; Block 3: [(6)].'], 'answer': 'Total Basic Blocks = 3.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In L-attributed SDT, actions can be placed anywhere, but they can only use values from elements to their LEFT!', 'Three-address code has at most 3 addresses per instruction (e.g. x = y op z).']}, ('OS', 0): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Process Management, CPU Scheduling & Threads', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Cooking Multiple Dishes on a 1-Burner Stove', 'text': '• CPU Scheduling: Like a chef with 1 stove burner (CPU) cooking 4 dishes (processes). FCFS finishes dish 1 completely before starting dish 2. Round Robin cooks each dish for 2 minutes, switching rapidly so no customer starves!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '26.1', 'title': 'The fork() System Call & Process Tree', 'content': "• <code>fork()</code> clones the calling process to create an exact child process.<br/>• <b>Return Value of fork():</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>0 to the Child process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>Child's PID (> 0) to the Parent process</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Returns <b>negative</b> on failure.<br/>• <b>Number of Processes Created:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If <code>fork()</code> is executed <b>n times</b> sequentially: Total processes = <b>2^n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Number of <b>Child processes</b> created = <b>2^n - 1</b>.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In short-circuit expressions like fork() && fork(): the second fork executes ONLY in the parent process because fork() returns 0 (False) to the child, short-circuiting the &&!'}, {'number': '26.2', 'title': 'CPU Scheduling Metrics & Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Turnaround Time (TAT):</b> Completion Time - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Waiting Time (WT):</b> Turnaround Time - Burst Time.<br/>• <b>Response Time:</b> First Time CPU is Allocated - Arrival Time.<br/>• <b>Key Algorithm Takeaways:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>SJF / SRTF:</b> Provably gives <b>minimum Average Waiting Time</b> among all scheduling algorithms!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Round Robin (RR):</b> Time quantum q. If q is very large, RR degenerates into FCFS; if q is tiny, context switch overhead dominates.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Convoy Effect:</b> Occurs in FCFS when a long CPU-bound process holds the CPU while short I/O-bound processes wait.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SRTF (Shortest Remaining Time First) is preemptive SJF and gives minimum average waiting time, but can cause starvation for long processes!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating Number of Created Processes', 'problem': 'How many total child processes are created by the code: for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) { fork(); } ?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Loop runs 3 times, executing fork() 3 times unconditionally.', 'Step 2: Formula for total processes = 2^n where n = 3 ⇒ 2^3 = 8 total processes.', 'Step 3: Total child processes = 2^n - 1 = 8 - 1 = 7 child processes.'], 'answer': 'Total child processes created = 7.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Do not confuse total processes (2^n) with total child processes (2^n - 1)!', 'Non-preemptive algorithms NEVER interrupt a process once it is running on the CPU.']}, ('OS', 1): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Process Synchronization, Semaphores & Deadlock Prevention', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Single-Key Restroom & Traffic Gridlock', 'text': '• Semaphore: A single restroom key at a gas station. Only the person holding the key can enter (Mutual Exclusion). Others must wait until the key is returned (Wait/Signal).\n• Deadlock: A 4-way intersection gridlock where every car is blocked by the car in front of it, and no one can move forward!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '27.1', 'title': 'Critical Section Requirements & Semaphores', 'content': '• <b>Critical Section (CS) Problem:</b> Region of code accessing shared resources.<br/>• <b>3 Mandatory Requirements:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion (Mandatory):</b> If process Pi is executing in CS, no other process can enter CS.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Progress (Mandatory):</b> Only processes currently competing to enter CS can participate in deciding who enters next.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>Bounded Waiting:</b> A bound must exist on the number of times others enter CS before a waiting process gets its turn.<br/>• <b>Counting Semaphores:</b> Initialized to N (available resources).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>wait(S)</code> (or P): decrements S. If S < 0, process blocks.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <code>signal(S)</code> (or V): increments S. If S ≤ 0, unblocks a waiting process.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If counting semaphore is initialized to S0, after p wait operations and v signal operations, final value = S0 - p + v. If final value is negative (-k), exactly k processes are blocked in the waiting queue!'}, {'number': '27.2', 'title': "Deadlock: 4 Necessary Conditions & Banker's Algorithm", 'content': "• <b>The 4 Coffman Conditions (Must ALL hold simultaneously for deadlock):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Mutual Exclusion:</b> Resources cannot be shared.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>Hold and Wait:</b> Process holds at least 1 resource while requesting others.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>No Preemption:</b> Resources cannot be forcibly taken away.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>Circular Wait:</b> A closed loop of processes waiting for resources held by the next.<br/>• <b>Banker's Algorithm:</b> Deadlock avoidance via Safe State analysis.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Need Matrix = Max Requirement - Current Allocation</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If available resources can satisfy Need of a process, it finishes and returns its allocation to Available.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: An unsafe state does NOT mean deadlock! Every deadlocked state is unsafe, but an unsafe state may or may not lead to deadlock.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Counting Semaphore Value and Blocked Processes', 'problem': 'A counting semaphore S is initialized to 10. Then 14 wait() operations and 3 signal() operations are executed on S. What is the final value of S, and how many processes are blocked?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Formula: Final S = Initial + Signals - Waits.', 'Step 2: Final S = 10 + 3 - 14 = 13 - 14 = -1.', 'Step 3: When S is negative, the magnitude |S| represents the number of blocked processes.', 'Step 4: | -1 | = 1 process is blocked.'], 'answer': 'Final value of S = -1; Exactly 1 process is blocked.'}], 'exam_traps': ["Peterson's algorithm works strictly for TWO processes, not general n processes without modifications!", 'Strict alternation (using only turn variable) satisfies Mutual Exclusion, but FAILS Progress.']}, ('OS', 2): {'sub_id': 'OS', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Paging, Virtual Memory, TLB & Inodes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Book Chapter Index & Speed Dial', 'text': "• Paging: Like dividing a 500-page novel into 10-page chapters so only the chapter you are currently reading needs to be open on your desk.\n• TLB: Like a smartphone speed-dial list storing your top 8 favorite contacts so you don't look up their numbers in the full directory each time."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '28.1', 'title': 'Paging & Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with TLB', 'content': '• <b>Virtual Address Split:</b> [ Page Number (p) | Page Offset (d) ].<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Offset = <b>log2(Page Size)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Page Number = Virtual Address Bits - Page Offset.<br/>• <b>Page Table Size:</b> (Number of Pages) × (Page Table Entry PTE Size).<br/>• <b>Effective Memory Access Time (EMAT) with 1-level Paging:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>EMAT = Hit_Rate × (t_{tlb} + t_m) + (1 - Hit_Rate) × (t_{tlb} + 2 × t_m)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;If TLB miss searches memory directly: EMAT = t_{tlb} + t_m + (1 - Hit_Rate) × t_m.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On a TLB hit, memory is accessed ONCE (for the data word). On a TLB miss in 1-level paging, memory is accessed TWICE (first for page table entry, second for actual data word)!'}, {'number': '28.2', 'title': "Page Replacement Algorithms & Belady's Anomaly", 'content': "• <b>FIFO (First-In First-Out):</b> Replaces the oldest loaded page.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Suffers from <b>Belady's Anomaly</b>: Increasing the number of page frames can INCREASE the number of page faults!<br/>• <b>LRU (Least Recently Used):</b> Replaces page not used for the longest time in the past. (Does NOT suffer from Belady's Anomaly; belongs to Stack Algorithms).<br/>• <b>Optimal Page Replacement (OPT / MIN):</b> Replaces page that will not be used for longest time in the FUTURE. (Provably minimum page faults, but impossible to implement in practice).", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Belady's Anomaly occurs in FIFO! Stack algorithms like LRU and Optimal NEVER suffer from Belady's Anomaly."}, {'number': '28.3', 'title': 'Unix Inodes & Maximum File Size', 'content': '• An Inode contains direct and indirect disk block pointers:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Direct Pointers (e.g. 12 pointers):</b> 12 × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Single Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size) × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Double Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)² × Block Size.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Triple Indirect:</b> (Block Size / Pointer Size)³ × Block Size.<br/>• <b>Max File Size = Sum of all addressable bytes across direct, single, double, and triple pointers!</b>', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Number of pointers per indirect block = Block Size / Disk Pointer Size. For 1 KB blocks and 4-byte pointers: 1024 / 4 = 256 pointers per block!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: EMAT Calculation with TLB', 'problem': 'A system has TLB access time of 20 ns and main memory access time of 100 ns. The TLB hit ratio is 90%. What is the effective memory access time EMAT?', 'steps': ['Step 1: On TLB hit: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 120 ns.', 'Step 2: On TLB miss: access TLB (20 ns) + access Memory for Page Table (100 ns) + access Memory for data (100 ns) = 220 ns.', 'Step 3: EMAT = (0.90 × 120 ns) + (0.10 × 220 ns).', 'Step 4: EMAT = 108 ns + 22 ns = 130 ns.'], 'answer': 'EMAT = 130 ns.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In multi-level paging with k levels: On TLB miss, you need k memory accesses for page tables + 1 memory access for data = (k + 1) memory accesses!', 'Internal fragmentation occurs on the last page of a process; paging completely eliminates external fragmentation.']}, ('DB', 0): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'ER-Model, Relational Algebra & SQL', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: University Enrollment & Excel Filters', 'text': '• ER Model: Blueprint of a university showing Students, Courses, and Departments with relationships (1:Many, Many:Many).\n• Relational Algebra: Filter (Select σ) picks specific rows; Column picker (Project π) hides unwanted columns; Join combines matching rows.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '29.1', 'title': 'ER to Relational Table Conversion Rules', 'content': "• <b>Converting Relationships to Tables:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>Many-to-Many (M:N):</b> Requires a separate relationship table whose primary key is the combination of primary keys of both participating entities.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>One-to-Many (1:N):</b> Can be combined into the 'Many' side table by placing the primary key of the '1' side as a Foreign Key (FK).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>One-to-One (1:1):</b> Can combine into either table, or merge both into a single table if total participation holds.<br/>• <b>Weak Entity:</b> Has no primary key of its own; identified using a discriminator (partial key) and owner's primary key. Always merged with its identifying relationship into 1 table.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 1:N relationship with partial participation on both sides, minimum number of tables is 2! In M:N relationship, minimum number of tables is ALWAYS 3 (Table A, Table B, and Relationship Table AB).'}, {'number': '29.2', 'title': 'Relational Algebra vs SQL Rules', 'content': '• <b>Relational Algebra Operators:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selection (σ_condition):</b> Filters rows that satisfy condition.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Projection (π_attributes):</b> Selects specified columns and <b>automatically eliminates duplicates</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Natural Join (⨝):</b> Cartesian product followed by equality selection on common attributes, then projection.<br/>• <b>SQL vs Relational Algebra:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Relational Algebra works on Sets (no duplicates).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- SQL operates on Multi-sets / Bags (duplicates preserved unless <code>DISTINCT</code> is specified).<br/>• <b>Three-Valued Logic in SQL:</b> Comparisons with <code>NULL</code> evaluate to <b>UNKNOWN</b>! <code>WHERE NULL = NULL</code> evaluates to UNKNOWN (returns 0 rows).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: π (projection) in Relational Algebra eliminates duplicates automatically, but SELECT in SQL preserves duplicates unless SELECT DISTINCT is explicitly written!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Minimum Relational Tables for ER Diagram', 'problem': 'An ER diagram has entities E1, E2, E3. Relationship R1 between E1 and E2 is M:N. Relationship R2 between E2 and E3 is 1:N. How many minimum tables are required in relational schema?', 'steps': ['Step 1: E1 table is created.', 'Step 2: E2 table is created.', 'Step 3: M:N relationship R1 requires a separate relationship table (R1_table).', 'Step 4: 1:N relationship R2 between E2 (1) and E3 (N): E3 can hold the foreign key of E2. Separate table for R2 is NOT needed.', 'Step 5: E3 table is created (with FK of E2).', 'Step 6: Total tables: E1, E2, R1_table, E3 = 4 tables.'], 'answer': 'Minimum number of tables = 4.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In SQL, COUNT(*) counts rows including NULLs; COUNT(column_name) ignores NULL rows!', 'HAVING clause filters groups AFTER aggregation, while WHERE filters individual rows BEFORE aggregation.']}, ('DB', 1): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Functional Dependencies, Normalization & B+ Trees', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Student Roll Number & Library Index Tree', 'text': "• Functional Dependency: Like your passport number uniquely determining your name and date of birth (Passport -> Name, DOB).\n• B+ Tree: The alphabetical thumb-tabs on a physical phonebook or dictionary, allowing you to jump to 'Smith' with just 3 page flips instead of reading all 50,000 names!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '30.1', 'title': 'The Step-by-Step Normal Forms Checklist', 'content': '• <b>1NF:</b> Every attribute contains strictly <b>atomic values</b> (no multi-valued or composite attributes).<br/>• <b>2NF:</b> Relation is in 1NF AND contains <b>NO Partial Dependency</b> (no non-prime attribute depends on a proper subset of any candidate key).<br/>• <b>3NF:</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Either <b>X is a Superkey</b>,<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• OR <b>Y is a Prime Attribute</b> (part of some candidate key).<br/>• <b>BCNF (Boyce-Codd Normal Form):</b> For every non-trivial FD <b>X → Y</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>X MUST BE A SUPERKEY!</b> (Strictly eliminates all transitive and partial dependencies).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 3NF, the RHS (Y) is allowed to be a prime attribute even if LHS (X) is not a superkey! In BCNF, this exception is REMOVED: LHS (X) MUST always be a superkey!'}, {'number': '30.2', 'title': 'Decomposition Properties: Lossless & Dependency Preserving', 'content': '• <b>Lossless Join Test (for binary decomposition R1, R2):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;R1 ⨝ R2 is Lossless IF AND ONLY IF common attribute is a superkey of at least one relation:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>(R1 ∩ R2) → R1 &nbsp;OR&nbsp; (R1 ∩ R2) → R2</b>.<br/>• <b>Decomposition Guarantees:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into 3NF</b> that is BOTH Lossless AND Dependency Preserving.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Any relation can <b>ALWAYS be decomposed into BCNF</b> that is Lossless, but Dependency Preservation is NOT guaranteed!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: BCNF decomposition guarantees Lossless Join, but may LOSE functional dependencies! 3NF decomposition guarantees BOTH Lossless Join AND Dependency Preservation.'}, {'number': '30.3', 'title': 'B-Tree vs B+ Tree Core Properties', 'content': '• <b>B-Tree:</b> Keys and data pointers are stored in BOTH internal and leaf nodes.<br/>• <b>B+ Tree:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Data pointers are stored <b>ONLY in Leaf Nodes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Leaf nodes are connected via a <b>singly/doubly linked list</b> for fast sequential range queries.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal nodes contain only search keys and child tree pointers (higher fanout!).<br/>• <b>Node Capacities (Order p):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Internal node holds at most <b>p pointers</b> and <b>p - 1 keys</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Minimum keys in internal node: <b>⌈p / 2⌉ - 1</b> (except root: min 1 key).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In B+ Trees, internal nodes only store routing keys (no data pointers)! Hence internal nodes fit far more keys per disk block than standard B-Trees, keeping tree height very small (typically 3 to 4).'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Finding Highest Normal Form', 'problem': 'Relation R(A, B, C, D) with FDs: { AB → C, C → D, D → A }. What is the highest normal form of R?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Compute candidate keys: (AB)+ = {A,B,C,D} ⇒ AB is a key. (BC)+ = {B,C,D,A} ⇒ BC is a key. (BD)+ = {B,D,A,C} ⇒ BD is a key.', 'Step 2: Candidate keys are AB, BC, BD. Prime attributes = { A, B, C, D } (all attributes are prime!).', 'Step 3: Check 2NF: No non-prime attributes exist ⇒ 2NF holds trivially!', 'Step 4: Check 3NF: For FD C → D: C is not superkey, but D is a prime attribute! For FD D → A: D is not superkey, but A is a prime attribute! Since RHS is always prime, 3NF holds!', 'Step 5: Check BCNF: In C → D, C is not a superkey. Fails BCNF!'], 'answer': 'Highest normal form is 3NF.'}], 'exam_traps': ['If ALL attributes in a relation are Prime Attributes, the relation is GUARANTEED to be in 3NF!', 'Order of B-tree p means maximum child pointers; maximum keys is always p - 1.']}, ('DB', 2): {'sub_id': 'DB', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Transactions, Serializability & Two-Phase Locking (2PL)', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: ATM Cash Withdrawal & Bank Balance Consistency', 'text': "• Atomicity: If an ATM dispenses ₹5,000 cash but power fails before updating your account balance, the system automatically rolls back so your money isn't lost.\n• Conflict Serializability: Making concurrent transactions execute as if they ran one after another in some serial order."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '31.1', 'title': 'Conflict Serializability & Precedence Graphs', 'content': '• <b>Two operations are in CONFLICT if:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. They belong to <b>different transactions</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. They access the <b>same data item</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>At least one of them is a WRITE</b> operation (Read-Write, Write-Read, or Write-Write).<br/>• <b>Precedence Graph (Testing Conflict Serializability):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Draw directed edge <b>Ti → Tj</b> if Ti executes a conflicting operation BEFORE Tj.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Testing Rule:</b> Schedule is Conflict Serializable <b>IF AND ONLY IF the precedence graph has NO CYCLES (Acyclic)!</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Equivalent serial order = <b>Topological Sort</b> of the graph.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If precedence graph contains a cycle (e.g. T1 → T2 → T1), it is 100% NOT conflict serializable! A schedule without cycles is always conflict serializable.'}, {'number': '31.2', 'title': 'Two-Phase Locking (2PL) Protocol', 'content': '• <b>Basic 2PL:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Growing Phase:</b> Transaction may acquire locks, but CANNOT release any lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Shrinking Phase:</b> Transaction may release locks, but CANNOT acquire any new lock.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Guarantees Conflict Serializability!</b> But DOES NOT prevent deadlocks or cascading aborts.<br/>• <b>Strict 2PL:</b> Holds all EXCLUSIVE (X) write locks until Commit/Abort. (Prevents cascading rollbacks!).<br/>• <b>Rigorous 2PL:</b> Holds ALL locks (shared and exclusive) until Commit/Abort.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Basic 2PL guarantees Conflict Serializability, but is NOT free from Deadlocks! Strict 2PL is Recoverable and Cascading-Free.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Conflict Serializability Cycle Detection', 'problem': 'Given schedule S: r1(X), r2(Y), w1(Y), w2(X), c1, c2. Is S conflict serializable?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Conflicting pairs on X: r1(X) by T1 and w2(X) by T2 ⇒ T1 executes before T2 ⇒ Edge T1 → T2.', 'Step 2: Conflicting pairs on Y: r2(Y) by T2 and w1(Y) by T1 ⇒ T2 executes before T1 ⇒ Edge T2 → T1.', 'Step 3: Graph contains edges T1 → T2 and T2 → T1, forming a directed cycle T1 ⇄ T2.', 'Step 4: Since precedence graph has a cycle, S is NOT conflict serializable.'], 'answer': 'Schedule S is NOT conflict serializable (contains cycle T1 ⇄ T2).'}], 'exam_traps': ['Every conflict serializable schedule is view serializable, but not vice versa! View serializability allows blind writes.', 'ACID: Durability guarantees that committed changes survive power failures using Write-Ahead Logging (WAL).']}, ('CN', 0): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'OSI/TCP-IP Layers, Framing & CRC', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Postal Mail Envelope & Barcode Checksum', 'text': '• Layered Architecture: Writing a letter (Application), translating it to Spanish (Presentation), putting it in an envelope (Transport), writing address (Network), placing in delivery truck (Data Link), sending along highway (Physical).\n• CRC: The checksum barcode on the back of an envelope that detects if a digit got smudged in the rain.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '32.1', 'title': 'CRC (Cyclic Redundancy Check) Polynomial Division', 'content': '• Let generator polynomial G(x) have degree <b>k</b> (i.e. k + 1 bits).<br/>• <b>Encoding Steps:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Append <b>k zeros</b> to the end of the original data frame.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Perform <b>Modulo-2 Binary Division</b> (using XOR instead of subtraction).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. The k-bit remainder is the <b>CRC Checksum</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. Transmitted frame = <b>Original Data + CRC Remainder</b>.<br/>• <b>Receiver Check:</b> Receiver divides received frame by G(x). If remainder is all 0s, frame is accepted.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: If generator polynomial has degree k, append exactly k zeros to data! Modulo-2 division uses XOR (0⊕0=0, 1⊕1=0, 0⊕1=1, 1⊕0=1). Never use arithmetic borrowing!'}, {'number': '32.2', 'title': 'Hamming Code & Error Correction', 'content': '• <b>Parity Bits Formula:</b> For m data bits and p parity bits:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>2^p ≥ m + p + 1</b>.<br/>• Parity bits placed at powers of 2 (positions 1, 2, 4, 8, ...).<br/>• <b>Hamming Distance (d):</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To detect up to <b>d errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>d + 1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• To correct up to <b>t errors</b>: Minimum Hamming distance = <b>2t + 1</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: To correct t single-bit errors, minimum distance d_min ≥ 2t + 1! To detect t errors, d_min ≥ t + 1.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Calculating CRC Transmitted Frame', 'problem': 'Given Data = 100100 and Generator polynomial G(x) = x³ + x² + 1 (binary 1101). Find the CRC remainder and transmitted bit sequence.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Degree of G(x) is 3 ⇒ Append 3 zeros to data: 100100 000.', 'Step 2: Divide 100100000 by 1101 using Modulo-2 XOR division:', '  100100000 XOR 110100000 = 010000000', '  010000000 XOR 011010000 = 001010000', '  001010000 XOR 001101000 = 000111000', '  000111000 XOR 000110100 = 000001100', '  000001100 XOR 000001101 = 000000001', 'Step 3: 3-bit remainder is 001.', 'Step 4: Transmitted codeword = 100100 001.'], 'answer': 'CRC remainder = 001; Transmitted frame = 100100001.'}], 'exam_traps': ["In bit stuffing: Sender inserts a '0' after FIVE consecutive 1s! Flag is 01111110 (six 1s).", 'Physical layer deals with bits, Data Link with frames, Network with packets, Transport with segments.']}, ('CN', 1): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Flow Control Protocols & CSMA/CD', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Walkie-Talkie & Crowded Dinner Party', 'text': "• Stop-and-Wait: Like a walkie-talkie conversation. You say 'Over' and wait until the other person acknowledges before speaking again.\n• CSMA/CD: Like people at a dinner party. You listen before speaking (Carrier Sense). If two speak at once (Collision), both stop, wait a random time (Backoff), and try again."}], 'foundations': [{'number': '33.1', 'title': 'Sliding Window Protocol Efficiency Formulas', 'content': '• <b>Key Parameters:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Transmission Time: <b>Tt = Frame Size (L) / Bandwidth (B)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Propagation Time: <b>Tp = Distance (d) / Propagation Velocity (v)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Parameter a: <b>a = Tp / Tt</b>.<br/>• <b>Efficiency Formulas:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Stop-and-Wait:</b> <b>η = 1 / (1 + 2a)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Sliding Window with window size W:</b> <b>η = W / (1 + 2a)</b> (capped at 100%).<br/>• <b>Window Sizes for k-bit sequence numbers:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Go-Back-N (GBN):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^k - 1</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>1</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Selective Repeat (SR):</b> Sender Window Ws = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>; Receiver Window Wr = <b>2^(k - 1)</b>.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In Selective Repeat (SR), Ws + Wr ≤ 2^k. Since Ws = Wr, maximum window size is 2^(k-1)! In GBN, Ws ≤ 2^k - 1 and Wr = 1.'}, {'number': '33.2', 'title': 'CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Size', 'content': '• <b>Collision Detection Condition:</b> Transmission time MUST be at least twice propagation time:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Tt ≥ 2 × Tp</b>.<br/>• <b>Minimum Frame Size Formula:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>L_min ≥ 2 × Tp × Bandwidth</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;= <b>2 × (Distance / Velocity) × Bandwidth</b>.<br/>• <b>Why 2 × Tp?</b> Because in the worst case, a collision can occur just before the signal reaches the destination, requiring another Tp for the collision signal to travel back to the sender!', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In CSMA/CD, L_min = 2 × Tp × Bandwidth! If frame is smaller than L_min, sender finishes transmitting before collision signal arrives, failing to detect collision.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: CSMA/CD Minimum Frame Length', 'problem': 'A 1 km network has bandwidth 1 Gbps and signal velocity 2 × 10^8 m/s. Calculate the minimum frame size for CSMA/CD.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Distance d = 1000 m; Velocity v = 2 × 10^8 m/s.', 'Step 2: Tp = d / v = 1000 / (2 × 10^8) = 5 × 10^(-6) seconds = 5 µs.', 'Step 3: Bandwidth B = 1 Gbps = 10^9 bits/sec.', 'Step 4: L_min = 2 × Tp × B = 2 × (5 × 10^(-6)) × 10^9 = 10 × 10^3 = 10,000 bits = 1250 bytes.'], 'answer': 'Minimum frame size = 10,000 bits (1250 bytes).'}], 'exam_traps': ['In GBN, out-of-order packets are silently discarded because receiver window size is strictly 1!', 'In Selective Repeat, independent acknowledgments are sent for each packet, requiring memory buffers at receiver.']}, ('CN', 2): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'IPv4, Subnetting, Routing & Fragmentation', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Splitting a Pizza & Oversized Cargo Slicing', 'text': '• Subnetting: Slicing a large pizza into smaller slices so each department gets exactly what they need without wasting slices.\n• Fragmentation: Like shipping a large sofa that cannot fit through the front door (MTU), so you unscrew it into 3 smaller pieces and reassemble inside!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '34.1', 'title': 'IPv4 Subnetting & Usable Hosts', 'content': '• In CIDR notation <b>a.b.c.d / n</b>:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Network bits = <b>n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Host bits = <b>32 - n</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Total IP addresses = <b>2^(32 - n)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Usable Host addresses = 2^(32 - n) - 2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;(Subtract 2: First is Network ID; Last is Directed Broadcast Address).<br/>• <b>Subnet Mask:</b> A 32-bit number with n leading 1s followed by (32 - n) zeros.<br/>• <b>Finding Subnet ID:</b> Bitwise AND between IP address and Subnet Mask.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In a /30 subnet: 2^(32-30) = 4 IPs. Minus 2 = exactly 2 usable host addresses (ideal for point-to-point router links)!'}, {'number': '34.2', 'title': 'Fragmentation Offset Calculation Rules', 'content': "• <b>Maximum Transmission Unit (MTU):</b> Max packet size physical link can carry.<br/>• <b>Key Fragmentation Header Fields:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>MF (More Fragments) Flag:</b> 1 for all fragments EXCEPT the last fragment (MF = 0 for last).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>DF (Don't Fragment) Flag:</b> If 1 and packet exceeds MTU, router drops packet and sends ICMP error.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Fragment Offset:</b> Offset field in IPv4 header is measured in units of <b>8 bytes</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Offset = (Byte offset of data) / 8</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Data payload in each fragment (except last) MUST be a multiple of 8 bytes!</b>", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Fragment offset is measured in units of 8 BYTES! Subtract the 20-byte IP header first: if MTU = 1500 bytes, max data payload is 1480 bytes (1480 / 8 = 185, a valid multiple of 8)!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: IPv4 Packet Fragmentation', 'problem': 'An IP datagram of 4000 bytes (including 20-byte IP header) arrives at a router where MTU = 1500 bytes. Determine the payload size, MF flag, and offset of each fragment.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Total data to transmit = 4000 - 20 = 3980 bytes.', 'Step 2: MTU = 1500 ⇒ Max payload per fragment = 1500 - 20 = 1480 bytes (multiple of 8).', 'Step 3: Fragment 1: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 0 / 8 = 0, MF = 1.', 'Step 4: Fragment 2: Payload = 1480 bytes, Offset = 1480 / 8 = 185, MF = 1.', 'Step 5: Remaining data = 3980 - (1480 + 1480) = 1020 bytes.', 'Step 6: Fragment 3: Payload = 1020 bytes, Offset = 2960 / 8 = 370, MF = 0.'], 'answer': 'Frag 1: 1480B, MF=1, Off=0; Frag 2: 1480B, MF=1, Off=185; Frag 3: 1020B, MF=0, Off=370.'}], 'exam_traps': ['Reassembly of fragments is performed ONLY at the DESTINATION host, never at intermediate routers!', 'Distance Vector routing suffers from Count-to-Infinity problem; solved by Split Horizon and Poison Reverse.']}, ('CN', 3): {'sub_id': 'CN', 'chap_idx': 3, 'title': 'TCP Congestion Control & Application Protocols', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Highway Speed Limit & Highway Traffic Jam', 'text': '• TCP Slow Start: Starting your car gently and doubling speed every block until you hit the threshold speed limit.\n• Congestion Avoidance: Once past the threshold, increasing speed by only 1 km/h each block to avoid crashing into traffic ahead!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '35.1', 'title': 'TCP Congestion Window Phases', 'content': '• <b>1. Slow Start Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Congestion Window (cwnd) starts at 1 MSS (Maximum Segment Size).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- For every ACK received, cwnd increases by 1 MSS (effectively <b>DOUBLES every RTT</b>: exponential growth).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Continues until cwnd reaches <b>ssthresh (slow-start threshold)</b>.<br/>• <b>2. Congestion Avoidance Phase:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- cwnd increases by <b>1 MSS per RTT</b> (additive increase: linear growth).<br/>• <b>3. On Loss Event:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>Timeout (Severe Loss):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd resets to 1 MSS</b> (re-enters Slow Start).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• <b>3 Duplicate ACKs (Mild Loss - Fast Retransmit):</b> <b>ssthresh = cwnd / 2</b>, and <b>cwnd = ssthresh</b> (Fast Recovery).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: On 3 duplicate ACKs, cwnd drops to ssthresh (not 1 MSS!) and enters Congestion Avoidance. On Timeout, cwnd resets all the way down to 1 MSS!'}, {'number': '35.2', 'title': 'Application Layer Protocols Summary', 'content': '• <b>DNS (Port 53):</b> Uses <b>UDP</b> for standard queries (fast); uses <b>TCP</b> for zone transfers.<br/>• <b>HTTP (Port 80) / HTTPS (Port 443):</b> Uses <b>TCP</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Non-persistent HTTP/1.0: 2 RTTs per object.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- Persistent HTTP/1.1: 1 RTT per object after initial connection handshake.<br/>• <b>Email Protocols:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>SMTP (Port 25):</b> Push protocol (client to mail server, server to server).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>POP3 (Port 110) / IMAP (Port 143):</b> Pull protocols (client downloading mail from server).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: SMTP is a PUSH protocol! POP3 and IMAP are PULL protocols! DNS uses UDP for queries but TCP for zone transfers.'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Tracing TCP cwnd Growth', 'problem': 'A TCP connection has ssthresh = 16 KB and MSS = 2 KB. Starting from cwnd = 2 KB, trace the value of cwnd after 5 RTTs (assuming no loss).', 'steps': ['Step 1: Round 0: cwnd = 2 KB.', 'Step 2: RTT 1 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 4 KB.', 'Step 3: RTT 2 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 8 KB.', 'Step 4: RTT 3 (Slow Start): cwnd doubles to 16 KB (reaches ssthresh).', 'Step 5: RTT 4 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases linearly by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 18 KB.', 'Step 6: RTT 5 (Congestion Avoidance): cwnd increases by 1 MSS (+2 KB) = 20 KB.'], 'answer': 'After 5 RTTs, cwnd = 20 KB.'}], 'exam_traps': ['TCP uses byte sequence numbers, NOT packet sequence numbers! If packet carries 100 bytes starting at seq 500, next packet has seq 600.', 'Silently discarding packets by UDP provides zero flow or congestion control.']}, ('GA', 0): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 0, 'title': 'Quantitative Aptitude - Work, Speed, Ratios & Percentages', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: Two Taps Filling a Bathtub', 'text': '• Work and Time: Tap A fills a tub in 10 minutes (rate = 1/10 per min); Tap B fills it in 15 minutes (rate = 1/15 per min). Working together, their speeds add up: 1/10 + 1/15 = 1/6, filling the tub in 6 minutes!'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '36.1', 'title': 'Time, Speed, Distance & Relative Speed', 'content': '• <b>Fundamental Formula:</b> <b>Distance = Speed × Time</b>.<br/>• <b>Unit Conversion:</b> <b>1 km/h = 5 / 18 m/s</b> &nbsp;and&nbsp; 1 m/s = 18 / 5 km/h.<br/>• <b>Average Speed:</b> <b>Total Distance / Total Time</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- If equal distances covered at speeds v1 and v2: <b>Average Speed = 2 · v1 · v2 / (v1 + v2)</b> (Harmonic Mean!).<br/>• <b>Relative Speed:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Opposite directions:</b> Relative Speed = <b>v1 + v2</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Moving in <b>Same direction:</b> Relative Speed = <b>|v1 - v2|</b>.<br/>• <b>Boats and Streams:</b> Downstream speed = <b>u + v</b>; Upstream speed = <b>u - v</b> (where u = boat speed, v = stream speed).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Average speed is NEVER the simple arithmetic mean (v1 + v2)/2! For equal distance intervals, average speed is always 2·v1·v2 / (v1 + v2).'}, {'number': '36.2', 'title': 'Time & Work (The LCM Efficiency Method)', 'content': '• <b>LCM Method:</b> Assume Total Work = <b>LCM of all individual completion times</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. Calculate 1-day efficiency = Total Work / Individual Days.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. Combined efficiency = Sum of individual efficiencies.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. Total Days = Total Work / Combined Efficiency.<br/>• <b>Pipes and Cisterns:</b> Inlet pipe adds positive work (+); Outlet / leak pipe subtracts negative work (-).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Using the LCM method eliminates messy fractions completely! If A takes 12 days and B takes 18 days: Total Work = LCM(12, 18) = 36 units. Efficiency of A = 3, B = 2. Together = 36 / 5 = 7.2 days!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Two Trains Crossing Each Other', 'problem': 'Two trains of lengths 120 m and 180 m travel in opposite directions on parallel tracks at 40 km/h and 50 km/h. How many seconds do they take to completely pass each other?', 'steps': ['Step 1: Total distance to cover = Sum of train lengths = 120 + 180 = 300 meters.', 'Step 2: Relative speed in opposite directions = 40 + 50 = 90 km/h.', 'Step 3: Convert speed to m/s: 90 × (5 / 18) = 25 m/s.', 'Step 4: Time taken = Distance / Speed = 300 m / (25 m/s) = 12 seconds.'], 'answer': 'Time taken = 12 seconds.'}], 'exam_traps': ['When a train crosses a pole or person, distance covered = length of train alone (pole has negligible width).', 'Successive percentage discount of x% and y% is NOT (x + y)%! Equivalent discount = x + y - (xy / 100)%.']}, ('GA', 1): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 1, 'title': 'Analytical & Spatial Reasoning - Blood Relations, Clocks & Cubes', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': "Real-Life Analogy: Rubik's Cube Slicing & Wall Clock Hands", 'text': "• Painted Cube: Like a 3x3x3 Rubik's cube painted red on outside. Only the 8 corner pieces have 3 painted faces! Center pieces inside the cube have 0 painted faces.\n• Clock Hands: At 12:00 both hands point straight up. By 12:30, the hour hand has already crept halfway toward 1 (15 degrees)!"}], 'foundations': [{'number': '37.1', 'title': 'Clock Angle Formula', 'content': '• Angle between Hour and Minute hand at H hours and M minutes:<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;<b>Angle θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |</b>.<br/>• <b>Speeds of Hands:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Minute hand speed: <b>6° per minute</b> (360° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Hour hand speed: <b>0.5° per minute</b> (30° / 60 min).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;• Relative speed: <b>5.5° per minute</b>.<br/>• <b>Coincidence (0°):</b> Hands coincide <b>22 times in 24 hours</b> (not 24 times, due to 11-12-1 overlap).', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Use θ = |30H - 5.5M|! If angle is greater than 180°, reflex angle is 360° - θ.'}, {'number': '37.2', 'title': 'Painted Cube Slicing Formulas', 'content': '• A large cube painted on all outside faces is cut into <b>n³ smaller equal cubes</b> (n cuts per edge):<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;1. <b>3 Faces Painted (Corners):</b> ALWAYS exactly <b>8 cubes</b> (for any n ≥ 2).<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;2. <b>2 Faces Painted (Edges):</b> <b>12 × (n - 2)</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;3. <b>1 Face Painted (Face Centers):</b> <b>6 × (n - 2)²</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;4. <b>0 Faces Painted (Inner Core):</b> <b>(n - 2)³</b>.<br/>• <b>Verification:</b> 8 + 12(n - 2) + 6(n - 2)² + (n - 2)³ = n³.', 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Regardless of the value of n, cubes with 3 faces painted is ALWAYS 8 (the 8 corners of the cube)!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Angle between Clock Hands', 'problem': 'Find the angle between the hour hand and minute hand of a clock at 3:40.', 'steps': ['Step 1: Here H = 3, M = 40.', 'Step 2: Formula: θ = | 30 × H - (11 / 2) × M |.', 'Step 3: 30 × 3 = 90.', 'Step 4: (11 / 2) × 40 = 11 × 20 = 220.', 'Step 5: θ = | 90 - 220 | = | -130 | = 130°.'], 'answer': 'Angle between hands = 130°.'}], 'exam_traps': ['In calendar problems, remember that century years like 1900 are NOT leap years unless divisible by 400 (2000 is a leap year)!', "In blood relations, never assume gender from a person's name unless explicitly stated or implied by relationship (e.g. 'sister', 'father')."]}, ('GA', 2): {'sub_id': 'GA', 'chap_idx': 2, 'title': 'Verbal Aptitude - Grammar, Concord & Critical Reasoning', 'real_life_examples': [{'title': 'Real-Life Analogy: The Orchestra Conductor & Courtroom Logic', 'text': '• Concord: The subject and verb must play in harmony (Singular subject takes singular verb).\n• Critical Reasoning: Distinguishing between a confirmed fact stated in a text versus an unproven assumption made by the author.'}], 'foundations': [{'number': '38.1', 'title': 'Subject-Verb Agreement (Concord) Rules', 'content': "• <b>Rule 1: Either/Or & Neither/Nor:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>CLOSER subject</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 'Neither the manager nor the <i>employees were</i> present.'<br/>• <b>Rule 2: As well as / Together with / Along with:</b> Verb agrees with the <b>FIRST subject</b>!<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 'The <i>captain</i>, along with his crew members, <b>was</b> honored.'<br/>• <b>Rule 3: Each, Every, Everyone, Neither (alone):</b> Strictly <b>SINGULAR</b>.<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;Example: 'Each of the students <b>has</b> completed the project.'", 'crucial_rule': "🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: In 'A as well as B', verb follows A! In 'Either A or B', verb follows B (the nearest subject)!"}, {'number': '38.2', 'title': 'Common Word Confusions in GATE', 'content': "• <b>Affect vs Effect:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Affect (Verb):</b> To influence. ('The weather affected his mood').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Effect (Noun):</b> The result/consequence. ('The policy had a good effect').<br/>• <b>Complement vs Compliment:</b><br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Complement:</b> Completes or enhances something ('Bread and butter complement each other').<br/>&nbsp;&nbsp;- <b>Compliment:</b> An expression of praise ('He paid her a nice compliment').<br/>• <b>Principal vs Principle:</b> Principal = head of school / main; Principle = fundamental truth / rule.", 'crucial_rule': '🎯 GOLDEN RULE FOR GATE: Affect is almost always an Action (Verb), Effect is an End-result (Noun). Remember RAVEN: Remember Affect Verb Effect Noun!'}], 'worked_examples': [{'title': 'Worked Example: Subject-Verb Agreement Selection', 'problem': "Choose the correct verb: 'Neither the professor nor his research students _____ (was / were) able to solve the puzzle.'", 'steps': ["Step 1: Identify conjunction: 'Neither ... nor ...'.", 'Step 2: Rule of Proximity: The verb agrees with the subject closer to it.', "Step 3: The closer subject is 'his research students' (Plural).", "Step 4: Plural subject requires plural verb 'were'."], 'answer': "Correct sentence: 'Neither the professor nor his research students were able to solve the puzzle.'"}], 'exam_traps': ["In reading comprehension, answers that use extreme words like 'always', 'never', 'only' are usually wrong unless explicitly in the text!", 'An assumption is an UNSTATED premise required for the conclusion to hold true.']}}
